    "html": "<h2>August 31 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for 5 historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 31</strong> across American network broadcasting history (1941–1954):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 31 Exhaustive Archival Inventory</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Characters</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Gildersleeve_1941_08_31.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>The Gildy Arrives At Summerfield</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Gildy Arrives in Summerfield&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WEAF)</strong><br>1941-08-31<br>6:30 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>Kraft</strong><br>Parkay margarine</td>\n<td><strong>Harold Peary</strong> (Throckmorton P. Gildersleeve), <strong>Lurene Tuttle</strong> (Marjorie), <strong>Walter Tetley</strong> (Leroy), <strong>Lillian Randolph</strong> (Birdie)</td>\n<td><strong>Leonard L. Levinson</strong> (Writer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Gildersleeve leaves the Girdle Works in Wistful Vista on the overnight sleeper for Summerfield, where his orphaned niece Marjorie and nephew Leroy wait for an uncle they have never met; after a berth mix-up on the train he means only to settle the estate, then stays to raise them.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Burns_Allen_1943_08_31.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Guest Frank Sinatra Get&#39;s Bad News, 1st Song - Dancing The Victory Polka</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Guest Frank Sinatra&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WABC)</strong><br>1943-08-31<br>9:00 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Lever Brothers</strong><br>Swan soap</td>\n<td><strong>George Burns</strong>, <strong>Gracie Allen</strong>, <strong>Bill Goodwin</strong> (Announcer), <strong>Felix Mills</strong> (Orchestra), <strong>Mel Blanc</strong>, <strong>Clarence Nash</strong>, <strong>Frank Sinatra</strong> (Guest)</td>\n<td><strong>Felix Mills</strong> (Music), <strong>Bill Goodwin</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;, &quot;Music&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Back from an eight-week army-camp tour, Gracie goes to Sinatra&#39;s house to warn him that George has decided to become a crooner and put Frankie out of a job; they trade songs, and the Swan Ten close with the Victory Polka.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Yukon_1949_08_31.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>The Minister&#39;s Missing Money</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Minister&#39;s Missing Money&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>ABC (WJZ)</strong><br>1949-08-31<br>5:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Quaker Oats</strong><br>Puffed Wheat &amp; Puffed Rice</td>\n<td><strong>Paul Sutton</strong> (Sergeant Preston), <strong>Yukon King</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Tom Dougall</strong> (Writer), <strong>George W. Trendle</strong> (Producer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Adventure&quot;, &quot;Western&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>A minister coming into Dawson is murdered for the building-fund cash in his bag; a young newspaperman&#39;s son switched look-alike suitcases with him on the steamer Portland at Skagway, and Preston and King must recover the money before the killer does.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Family_Theater_1949_08_31.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>The Denver Express</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Denver Express&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mutual (WOR)</strong><br>1949-08-31<br>9:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Family Rosary / Holy Cross Family Theater</td>\n<td><strong>Lizabeth Scott</strong> (Hostess), <strong>Regis Toomey</strong> (Major Harry Sinclair), <strong>Jean Ruth</strong></td>\n<td>Family Theater (Hollywood)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Historical&quot;, &quot;Adventure&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>On the first run of the Denver Express, engine No. 17, Major Sinclair—who will not use a gun—is hemmed in by Big Jeff Johnson and the gamblers who want the railroad stopped, and has to get the train through without becoming the man he refuses to be.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Fibber_1954_08_31.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Fishing Boat Sinks</code>, <code>Robinson Crusoe McGee</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Fishing Boat Sinks&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WNBC)</strong><br>1954-08-31<br>10:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Lewis-Howe</strong><br>Tums</td>\n<td><strong>Jim Jordan</strong> (Fibber McGee), <strong>Marian Jordan</strong> (Molly), <strong>Arthur Q. Bryan</strong> (Doc Gamble), <strong>Harlow Wilcox</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><strong>Phil Leslie &amp; Ralph Goodman</strong> (Writers), <strong>Max Hutto</strong> (Director)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>After their fishing boat sinks, Fibber, Molly, and Doc Gamble wake stiff on an island in Lake Winnahoppa, eighty miles from Wistful Vista, and have to get off before Fibber&#39;s Robinson Crusoe schemes make the rescue worse.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 31</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 5</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 31</strong>: 5</li>\n<li><strong>Deferred</strong>: Archie Andrews (<code>Trying To Listen To A Baseball Game</code>) — circulating date conflicts with a Sunday 1951 logging; not stamped to 1946-08-31</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
