    "html": "<h2>August 27 Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 219)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the historical provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, and pure narrative synopses for broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 27</strong> across American network radio, Paul Whiteman conducting jazz standards on <em>Forever Tops</em>, Ronald Colman presenting Victor Hugo&#39;s <em>The Bishop&#39;s Candlesticks</em> and Sarah Grand&#39;s <em>When the Door Opened</em> on <em>Favorite Story</em>, Frank Graham as private eye <em>Jeff Regan</em>, Jesse L. Lasky&#39;s <em>The Gateway to Hollywood</em>, and William Bendix launching <em>The Houseboat Vacation</em> on <em>The Life of Riley</em> for the American Meat Institute (1939–1950):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 27 Archival Inventory (Increment 219)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Paul_Whiteman_1948.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Fun On The Telephone</code>, <code>1948</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Fun on the Telephone&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>ABC (WJZ)</strong><br>1948-08-27<br>9:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Musical Variety</td>\n<td><strong>Paul Whiteman</strong>, <strong>Mindy Carson</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Whiteman Rhythm Boys</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Jazz&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;, &quot;Pop Standards&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Paul Whiteman leads his orchestra in jazz arrangements and novelty tunes while chatting with listeners over live studio phone connections.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Favorite_Story_1946.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Bishop&#39;s Candlesticks</code>, <code>Hugo</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Bishop&#39;s Candlesticks (Les Misérables)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Syndicated (Ziv)</strong><br>1946-08-27</td>\n<td><strong>Commercial</strong><br>Literary Anthology</td>\n<td><strong>Ronald Colman</strong> (Host/Narrator)</td>\n<td><strong>Lawrence &amp; Lee</strong> (Writers)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Classic Literature&quot;, &quot;French&quot;, &quot;Inspirational&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>An impoverished ex-convict sheltered by a compassionate bishop steals the parish silver candlesticks, only to be saved from re-arrest by the bishop&#39;s mercy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Favorite_Story_1949.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>When the Door Opened</code>, <code>Colman</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;When the Door Opened&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Syndicated (Ziv)</strong><br>1949-08-27</td>\n<td><strong>Commercial</strong><br>Literary Anthology</td>\n<td><strong>Ronald Colman</strong>, <strong>Benita Hume</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Lawrence &amp; Lee</strong> (Writers)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Romance&quot;, &quot;Literature&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>A suspicious husband returns unexpectedly to his country estate after midnight and discovers an open front door leading to a bewildering romantic riddle.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Jeff_Regan_1950.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Investigator Gentlemen Prefer Horses</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Gentlemen Prefer Horses&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (KNX)</strong><br>1950-08-27<br>8:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Detective Mystery</td>\n<td><strong>Frank Graham</strong> (Regan), <strong>Frank Nelson</strong> (Lyon)</td>\n<td><strong>E. Jack Neuman</strong> (Writer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Hardboiled&quot;, &quot;Noir&quot;, &quot;Horse Racing&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Hardboiled private investigator Jeff Regan is hired to guard a prized thoroughbred racehorse after its trainer receives death threats from underworld bookies.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Gateway_Hollywood_1939.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>For Tonight Only</code>, <code>Lasky 1939</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;For Tonight Only&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WABC)</strong><br>1939-08-27<br>6:30 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>Wm. Wrigley Jr.</strong><br>Doublemint Gum</td>\n<td><strong>Jesse L. Lasky</strong> (Host), <strong>RKO Finalists</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Jesse L. Lasky Production</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Hollywood&quot;, &quot;Talent Search&quot;, &quot;Showcase&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Producer Jesse L. Lasky introduces two aspiring young contest finalists in an original dramatic showcase to win RKO Radio Pictures screen contracts.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Life_of_Riley_1944.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>The Houseboat Vacation Part 1</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Houseboat Vacation (Part 1)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Blue (WJZ)</strong><br>1944-08-27<br>3:00 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>American Meat Inst.</strong><br>Meat for the War Effort</td>\n<td><strong>William Bendix</strong>, <strong>Paula Winslowe</strong>, <strong>John Brown</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Irving Brecher</strong> (Creator)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Sitcom&quot;, &quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Domestic&quot;, &quot;Family&quot;, &quot;Americana&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Chester A. Riley rents an old, dilapidated houseboat for the family&#39;s summer vacation, only for the vessel to drift away downriver while they are asleep.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 27</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 27</strong>: 227</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
