    "html": "<h2>August 26 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 168)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, serial multi-part narrative arcs, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 26</strong> across American and British radio, Roy Acuff and Minnie Pearl live from the Ryman Auditorium on the <em>Grand Ole Opry</em> for Prince Albert Tobacco, Lloyd Nolan as hardboiled detective Martin Kane for Sano Cigarettes, Paul Whiteman celebrating Georgia Gibbs&#39; birthday on the <em>Philco Radio Hall of Fame</em>, <em>The Flying Doctor</em> in Australia, Gerald Mohr on <em>Crime Is My Pastime</em>, and <em>The Texas Rangers</em> (1945–1959):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 26 Archival Inventory (Increment 168)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Texas_Rangers_1950.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>The Open Range</code>, <code>Open Range Texas Rangers</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Open Range &amp; Western Ballads (The Texas Rangers — KMBC Transcriptions)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Syndicated</strong><br>1950-08-26<br>(KMBC Series)</td>\n<td><strong>Local Merchants</strong><br>Western Feed Mills</td>\n<td><strong>The Texas Rangers</strong> (Herb Kinsley, Hugh Beaumont)</td>\n<td><strong>Arthur B. Church</strong>, <strong>Gomer Cool</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Western&quot;, &quot;Country&quot;, &quot;Americana&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;, &quot;Folk&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;The Texas Rangers Show, direct from Kansas City!&quot; The famed Western singing ensemble performs cowboy harmonies and western swing favorites including &#39;The Open Range&#39;, &#39;Tumbling Tumbleweeds&#39;, and square-dance fiddle breakdowns.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Philco_Hall_Fame_1945.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Philco Presents 1945-08-26</code>, <code>Georgia Gibbs Birthday</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Georgia Gibbs&#39; Birthday Celebration (Paul Whiteman &amp; The Merry Macs — Philco)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Blue / ABC</strong><br>1945-08-26<br>6:00 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Philco Corporation</strong><br>Philco Radios &amp; Freezers</td>\n<td><strong>Paul Whiteman</strong>, <strong>Georgia Gibbs</strong>, <strong>The Merry Macs</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Willson M. Tuttle</strong>, <strong>Glenn Riggs</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Variety&quot;, &quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;, &quot;Jazz&quot;, &quot;Comedy&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Philco, famous for quality the world over, presents the Philco Radio Hall of Fame!&quot; Paul Whiteman and the Hall of Fame Orchestra throw a gala on-air birthday celebration for vocal star Georgia Gibbs, featuring four-part swing harmonies by The Merry Macs and rustic Arkansas humor from Bob Burns for Philco.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Crime_My_Pastime_1945.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Crime Is My Pastime 1945-08-26</code>, <code>The Fatal Flamingo</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Case of the Fatal Flamingo (Gerald Mohr — Crime Is My Pastime)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Syndicated</strong><br>1945-08-26<br>(Ziv Transcription)</td>\n<td><strong>Local Merchants</strong><br>Crime &amp; Intrigue Series</td>\n<td><strong>Gerald Mohr</strong> (Criminologist), <strong>Jeff Corey</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Jerry Devine</strong>, <strong>Frank Martin</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Hardboiled&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;, &quot;Short Form&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Crime Is My Pastime! True accounts of daring criminal schemes unmasked by criminological science!&quot; A debonair society criminologist (Gerald Mohr) investigates the poisoned drink death of an emerald dealer at a plush Miami nightclub, uncovering a counterfeit jewel smuggling ring disguised inside ornamental flamingo statues.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Flying_Doctor_1959.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Episode</code>, <code>The Outback Typhoid Outbreak</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Outback Typhoid Outbreak (James McKechnie &amp; Bill Kerr — BBC Flying Doctor)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Light</strong><br>1959-08-26<br>7:30 PM</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Outback Medical Drama</td>\n<td><strong>James McKechnie</strong> (Dr. Halliday), <strong>Bill Kerr</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Ernest Dudley</strong>, <strong>Charles Maxwell</strong>, <strong>David Jacobs</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Adventure&quot;, &quot;Medical&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Australian&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;The Flying Doctor, based on the real-life adventures of Australia&#39;s Royal Flying Doctor Service!&quot; Dr. Greg Halliday (James McKechnie) flies his emergency medical aircraft through a blinding desert dust storm to reach an isolated sheep station where a sudden typhoid epidemic threatens the lives of sheep shearers and children.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Martin_Kane_1951.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Woman in Pink</code>, <code>The Woman in Pink</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Case of the Woman in Pink (Lloyd Nolan as Martin Kane — Sano Cigarettes)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WEAF)</strong><br>1951-08-26<br>4:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>U.S. Tobacco Co.</strong><br>Sano Cigarettes &amp; Model</td>\n<td><strong>Lloyd Nolan</strong> (Martin Kane), <strong>Horace McMahon</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Ted Hediger</strong>, <strong>Frank Burns</strong>, <strong>Fred Collins</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Hardboiled&quot;, &quot;Noir&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;United States Tobacco Company presents Martin Kane, Private Investigator, starring Lloyd Nolan!&quot; Broadway private eye Martin Kane is hired by an elusive woman wearing a distinctive pink hat who vanishes minutes later, leaving Kane to solve a jewel robbery and a Midtown theater alley shooting before police lieutenant Happy Kowalski arrests him for the crime for Sano Cigarettes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Grand_Ole_Opry_1950.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Grand Ole Opry 1950-08-26</code>, <code>Roy Acuff Smoky Mtn</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Roy Acuff &amp; The Smoky Mountain Boys (Minnie Pearl — Prince Albert Grand Ole Opry)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WSM)</strong><br>1950-08-26<br>10:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>R.J. Reynolds</strong><br>Prince Albert Smoking Tobacco</td>\n<td><strong>Roy Acuff</strong>, <strong>Smoky Mountain Boys</strong>, <strong>Minnie Pearl</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Jack Stapp</strong>, <strong>Grant Turner</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Country&quot;, &quot;Americana&quot;, &quot;Folk&quot;, &quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Prince Albert, the National Joy Smoke, presents the Grand Ole Opry, live from the Ryman Auditorium in Nashville, starring the King of Country Music, Roy Acuff!&quot; Roy Acuff and the Smoky Mountain Boys perform &#39;Wabash Cannonball&#39; and &#39;Great Speckled Bird&#39;, alongside country humor from Minnie Pearl and Rod Brasfield for Prince Albert Pipe Tobacco.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 26</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 26</strong>: 233</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
