    "html": "<h2>August 25 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 78)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, serial multi-part narrative arcs, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 25</strong> across American network, superhero audio adventures, psychological horror starring Joseph Cotten, rare 1955 CBS Hollywood audition pilots starring George Raft, police procedurals, Nashville vocal variety, and early Depression-era domestic humor (1930–1960):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 25 Archival Inventory (Increment 78)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Official_Detective_1955.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Too Pretty to Live</code>, <code>Murdered Model Britt</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Too Pretty to Live (Craig McDonnell as Inspector Britt — Mutual)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mutual (WOR)</strong><br>1955-08-25<br>8:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Police Procedural</td>\n<td><strong>Craig McDonnell</strong> (Dan Britt), <strong>Martin Blaine</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Wynn Wright</strong> (Director), <strong>Carl Warren</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Procedural&quot;, &quot;Police&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;From the files of Official Detective Stories magazine...&quot; Detective Inspector Dan Britt investigates the strangulation murder of a glamorous photographic model found in her locked apartment, piecing together clues from a torn lipstick-stained receipt.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Haunting_Hour_1945.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Occupation Murder</code>, <code>Joseph Cotten Haunting Hour</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Occupation: Murder (Starring Joseph Cotten — NBC Transcription)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Synd.</strong><br>1945-08-25<br>(Haunting Hour)</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Psychological Horror</td>\n<td><strong>Joseph Cotten</strong> (Lead), <strong>Berry Kroeger</strong>, <strong>Betty Garde</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Walter McGraw</strong> (Director), <strong>Frank Gallup</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Horror&quot;, &quot;Psychological&quot;, &quot;Suspense&quot;, &quot;Macabre&quot;, &quot;Noir&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;It is the Haunting Hour... when the clock strikes twelve!&quot; Joseph Cotten stars as a cold-blooded criminologist who plots what he believes is the perfect untraceable homicide of his wealthy business partner, only to be driven mad by phantom typewriter keystrokes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Rocky_Jordan_1955_Take2.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Spring Of Moses Take 2</code>, <code>George Raft Rocky Jordan Pilot 2</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Spring of Moses (Audition Pilot Take 2 Starring George Raft — CBS)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (Audition)</strong><br>1955-08-25<br>(Columbia Square)</td>\n<td><strong>Audition Recording</strong><br>Proposed CBS Revival</td>\n<td><strong>George Raft</strong> (Rocky Jordan), <strong>Jay Novello</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Larry Roman</strong>, <strong>Cliff Howell</strong>, <strong>Roy Rowan</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Hardboiled&quot;, &quot;Noir&quot;, &quot;Exotic&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Rocky Jordan, owner of the Café Tambourine in Cairo...&quot; In this second 1955 CBS audition pilot take starring George Raft, Rocky Jordan navigates Cairo&#39;s deadly bazaars to protect an ancient biblical relic from a syndicate of ruthless antiquities smugglers.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Breakfast_Club_1960.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Anita Kerr Singers</code>, <code>Don McNeill Toni 1960</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Live with The Anita Kerr Singers (Don McNeill — Toni Home Permanents)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>ABC (WLS)</strong><br>1960-08-25<br>9:00 AM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>The Toni Company</strong><br>Toni Home Permanents</td>\n<td><strong>Don McNeill</strong> (Host), <strong>The Anita Kerr Singers</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Cliff Petersen</strong>, <strong>Bob Murphy</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Variety&quot;, &quot;Talk&quot;, &quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;, &quot;Morning&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Good morning, Breakfast Clubbers!&quot; Don McNeill and Aunt Fanny lead morning March Around the Breakfast Table hijinks, welcoming Grammy-winning vocal group The Anita Kerr Singers to perform lush Nashville pop harmonies for Toni Home Permanents.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Cecil_Sally_1930.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Spend Fifteen Dollars</code>, <code>Cecil Sally White King</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Cecil Plans How to Spend Fifteen Dollars (Cecil and Sally — White King Soap)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>KPO San Fran.</strong><br>1930-08-25<br>7:30 PM PST</td>\n<td><strong>Los Angeles Soap Co.</strong><br>White King Granulated</td>\n<td><strong>Johnny O&#39;Brien</strong> (Cecil), <strong>Helen Troy</strong> (Sally)</td>\n<td><strong>Johnny O&#39;Brien &amp; Helen Troy</strong>, <strong>Al Pearce</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Sitcom&quot;, &quot;Domestic&quot;, &quot;Early Radio&quot;, &quot;Dialogue&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Young couple Cecil and Sally bicker over Cecil&#39;s ambitious plans to spend a surprise fifteen-dollar bonus on a secondhand phonograph instead of household groceries for White King Granulated Soap.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Superman_1941_08_25.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Dr Roebling Voice Machine</code>, <code>Superman Kelloggs Pep</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Dr. Roebling &amp; the Voice Machine (Part 1 — Bud Collyer as Superman — Kellogg&#39;s Pep)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mutual (WOR)</strong><br>1941-08-25<br>5:15 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>The Kellogg Co.</strong><br>Kellogg&#39;s Pep Cereal</td>\n<td><strong>Bud Collyer</strong> (Clark Kent / Superman), <strong>Joan Alexander</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Jerry Siegel &amp; Joe Shuster</strong>, <strong>Jackson Beck</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Superhero&quot;, &quot;Adventure&quot;, &quot;Juvenile&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;, &quot;Science Fiction&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Faster than a speeding bullet! More powerful than a locomotive! Able to leap tall buildings in a single bound!&quot; In Part 1 of <em>Dr. Roebling and the Voice Machine</em>, Clark Kent and Lois Lane investigate a mad scientist whose ultrasonic soundwave invention can hypnotize entire city populations and shatter concrete skyscrapers for Kellogg&#39;s Pep Cereal.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 25</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 25</strong>: 83</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
