    "html": "<h2>August 25 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 74)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, serial multi-part narrative arcs, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 25</strong> across American network, progressive jazz concert masterworks, Pulitzer-caliber newspaper reporting drama, classic fairy tale theater, waterfront police procedurals, early 1930s slapstick comedy, and daytime dramatic romance (1931–1959):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 25 Archival Inventory (Increment 74)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Big_Story_1948_08_25.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Million Dollar Story</code>, <code>Ella Fleishman Big Story</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Million Dollar Swindle: Ella Fleishman of The Omaha World-Herald (Pall Mall)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WNBC)</strong><br>1948-08-25<br>10:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>American Tobacco Co.</strong><br>Pall Mall Famous Cigarettes</td>\n<td><strong>Robert Sloane</strong> (Narrator), <strong>Joan Alexander</strong>, <strong>Santos Ortega</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Bernard J. Prockter</strong>, <strong>Harry Ingram</strong>, <strong>Ernest Chappell</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Procedural&quot;, &quot;Docudrama&quot;, &quot;Journalism&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Tonight, to Ella Fleishman of The Omaha World-Herald, goes the Pall Mall award of five hundred dollars for a notable journalistic achievement!&quot; Ace reporter Ella Fleishman exposes a million-dollar Midwest real estate confidence swindle preying on elderly widows for Pall Mall.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Stan_Kenton_1953.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Innovations Modern Music</code>, <code>Stan Kenton NBC 1953</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Innovations in Modern Music (Stan Kenton &amp; His Orchestra — NBC)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WNBC)</strong><br>1953-08-25<br>9:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Progressive Jazz Showcase</td>\n<td><strong>Stan Kenton Orchestra</strong>, <strong>June Christy</strong> (Vocalist)</td>\n<td><strong>Bill Russo &amp; Pete Rugolo</strong>, <strong>NBC Music</strong>, <strong>Don Pardo</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Jazz&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;, &quot;Progressive Jazz&quot;, &quot;Concert&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Visionary progressive jazz innovator Stan Kenton leads his legendary 21-piece orchestra, performing complex modern compositions including &#39;Artistry in Rhythm&#39; and &#39;City of Glass&#39;, discussing progressive orchestration techniques with vocalist June Christy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Black_and_Blue_1931.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Stolen Diamond Collar</code>, <code>Black and Blue CBS 1931</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Stolen Diamond Collar (F. Chase Taylor &amp; Budd Hulick — CBS)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WABC)</strong><br>1931-08-25<br>7:15 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Early Comedy Serial</td>\n<td><strong>F. Chase Taylor</strong> (Black), <strong>Budd Hulick</strong> (Blue)</td>\n<td><strong>F. Chase Taylor</strong> (Writer), <strong>Frank Knight</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Satire&quot;, &quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Slapstick&quot;, &quot;Early Radio&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Bumbling private investigators Black and Blue (early radio pioneer F. Chase Taylor and Budd Hulick) stumble through a riotous jewel heist case, confusing every clue and tripping over each other in an effort to recover a dowager&#39;s diamond necklace.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>NBC_Theater_1959.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Princess Anne Thrushbeard</code>, <code>Alexander Scourby Grimm</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;King Thrushbeard (Starring Alexander Scourby &amp; Marian Seldes — NBC)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WRCA)</strong><br>1959-08-25<br>8:05 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Classic Drama Series</td>\n<td><strong>Alexander Scourby</strong>, <strong>Marian Seldes</strong>, <strong>Donald Buka</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Brothers Grimm</strong>, <strong>Kenneth MacGregor</strong>, <strong>Fred Collins</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Fairy Tale&quot;, &quot;Literature&quot;, &quot;Romance&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>A lavish radio dramatization of the classic Brothers Grimm fairy tale: when a haughty and vain royal princess scorns every suitor, she is married to a humble traveling minstrel (King Thrushbeard in disguise) to learn humility and true love.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Under_Arrest_1946.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Waterfront Shakedown</code>, <code>Captain Drake Under Arrest</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Waterfront Protection Shakedown (Craig McDonnell as Captain Drake — Mutual)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mutual (WOR)</strong><br>1946-08-25<br>5:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Police Procedural</td>\n<td><strong>Craig McDonnell</strong> (Jim Drake), <strong>Vicki Vola</strong></td>\n<td><strong>William Sweets</strong> (Dir.), <strong>Jerry McGill</strong>, <strong>Carl Warren</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Procedural&quot;, &quot;Police&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Under Arrest! The drama of police action against crime!&quot; Police Captain Jim Drake and the homicide squad launch an undercover operation to smash an extortion and protection racket terrorizing merchant sailors along the metropolitan docks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Hearts_Harmony_1947.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Penny Reports Boss</code>, <code>Penny Ross Kroger Bread</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Penny Reports to Her Boss (Jone Allison as Penny Ross — Kroger Bread)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Syndicated</strong><br>1947-08-25<br>(Ziv Radio)</td>\n<td><strong>The Kroger Co.</strong><br>Country Club Bread</td>\n<td><strong>Jone Allison</strong> (Penny Ross), <strong>Michael Fitzmaurice</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Frederic W. Ziv Company</strong>, <strong>Ed Herlihy</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Soap Opera&quot;, &quot;Romance&quot;, &quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Domestic&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Aspiring commercial singer Penny Ross reports for her first critical assignment at the Manhattan advertising agency, navigating workplace jealousy and romantic sparks with executive Barry Winters for Kroger Bread.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 25</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 25</strong>: 59</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
