    "html": "<h2>August 25 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 72)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, serial multi-part narrative arcs, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 25</strong> across American network, operetta variety showcases, studio rehearsal session recordings, serialized children&#39;s Oscar Wilde classics, concert romance broadcasts, and satirical morning sketches (1940–1952):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 25 Archival Inventory (Increment 72)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Kraft_1948_08_25_Rehearsal.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Get Happy Rehearsal</code>, <code>Nelson Eddy Studio 1948</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Studio Rehearsal: &#39;Get Happy&#39; (Guest Host Nelson Eddy — Kraft Music Hall)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (Studio A)</strong><br>1948-08-25<br>(Dress Rehearsal)</td>\n<td><strong>Kraft Foods Co.</strong><br>Miracle Whip &amp; Velveeta</td>\n<td><strong>Nelson Eddy</strong> (Baritone), <strong>Armbruster Orchestra</strong></td>\n<td><strong>J. Walter Thompson</strong>, <strong>Earl Ebi</strong>, <strong>Ken Carpenter</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;, &quot;Rehearsal&quot;, &quot;Comedy&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>An extraordinary behind-the-scenes recording capturing golden age baritone star Nelson Eddy and the Robert Armbruster Orchestra rehearsing Harold Arlen&#39;s &#39;Get Happy&#39; and trading spontaneous studio banter before the live Kraft Music Hall transmission.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Kraft_1949_08_25.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Nelson Eddy Dorothy Kirsten</code>, <code>Kraft Music Hall 1949</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Guest Host Nelson Eddy &amp; Metropolitan Soprano Dorothy Kirsten (Kraft Music Hall)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WNBC)</strong><br>1949-08-25<br>9:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Kraft Foods Co.</strong><br>Miracle Whip &amp; Mayonnaise</td>\n<td><strong>Nelson Eddy</strong> (Baritone), <strong>Dorothy Kirsten</strong></td>\n<td><strong>J. Walter Thompson</strong>, <strong>Earl Ebi</strong>, <strong>Ken Carpenter</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;, &quot;Classical&quot;, &quot;Operetta&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Baritone idol Nelson Eddy guest-hosts the <em>Kraft Music Hall</em>, reuniting with Metropolitan Opera soprano Dorothy Kirsten for a radiant performance of operetta duets and American folk songs for Kraft Miracle Whip.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Melody_Hour_1947.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Youre The Tops 1947</code>, <code>Jane Pickens Bayer Aspirin</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;You&#39;re the Top (Jane Pickens &amp; Bob Hannon — Bayer Aspirin)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WCBS)</strong><br>1947-08-25<br>8:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sterling Drug Inc.</strong><br>Bayer Aspirin &amp; Phillips&#39;</td>\n<td><strong>Jane Pickens</strong> (Soprano), <strong>Bob Hannon</strong> (Tenor)</td>\n<td><strong>Frank &amp; Anne Hummert</strong>, <strong>Frank Black</strong>, <strong>Ford Bond</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Classical&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;, &quot;Pop&quot;, &quot;Concert&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;The American Melody Hour, dedicated to the favorite songs that America loves!&quot; Jane Pickens and tenor Bob Hannon lead an enchanting half-hour concert featuring Cole Porter&#39;s &#39;You&#39;re the Top&#39; and Victor Herbert romances with Frank Black&#39;s Orchestra for Bayer Aspirin.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Canterville_Ghost_Pt1.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Canterville Ghost Pt 1of5</code>, <code>Oscar Wilde John Griggs</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Canterville Ghost: The Haunted Manor (Part 1 of 5 — John Griggs — Mutual)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mutual (WOR)</strong><br>1947-08-25<br>4:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Children&#39;s Literature</td>\n<td><strong>John Griggs</strong> (Solo Storyteller / All Voices)</td>\n<td><strong>Oscar Wilde</strong> (Author), <strong>Robert E. Morrison</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Adventure&quot;, &quot;Juvenile&quot;, &quot;Literature&quot;, &quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Supernatural&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Master storyteller John Griggs opens a five-part serialization of Oscar Wilde&#39;s witty classic <em>The Canterville Ghost</em>, detailing what happens when practical American Minister Hiram B. Otis purchases an ancient English manor haunted by Sir Simon de Canterville.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Family_Robinson_1940.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Aunt Agatha Baron Hike</code>, <code>Family Robinson NAM</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Aunt Agatha and the Baron&#39;s Mountain Hike (Our American Family Robinson — NAM)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Syndicated</strong><br>1940-08-25<br>(NAM Series)</td>\n<td><strong>National Assoc. of Mfrs.</strong><br>Free Enterprise &amp; Industry</td>\n<td><strong>Carlton Young</strong>, <strong>Colleen Ward</strong>, <strong>Ethel Wilson</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Grace Gibson Productions</strong>, <strong>Ed Prentiss</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Soap Opera&quot;, &quot;Domestic&quot;, &quot;Americana&quot;, &quot;Family&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Outspoken Aunt Agatha, daughter Myra, and the mysterious European aristocrat &#39;The Baron&#39; embark on a scenic summer mountain hike, sparking humorous cultural debates on American free enterprise versus Old World nobility.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Bob_and_Ray_1952.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Mary Backstayge Stock</code>, <code>Bob and Ray 1952</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Mary Backstayge&#39;s Summer Stock Disaster (Bob and Ray — NBC)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WNBC)</strong><br>1952-08-25<br>5:45 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Satirical Comedy</td>\n<td><strong>Bob Elliott</strong>, <strong>Ray Goulding</strong> (Wally Ballou)</td>\n<td><strong>Bob Elliott &amp; Ray Goulding</strong>, <strong>Arthur Van Horn</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Satire&quot;, &quot;Parody&quot;, &quot;Improvisation&quot;, &quot;Sketch&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Matinee with Bob and Ray!&quot; Comedy satirists Bob Elliott and Ray Goulding present another hilarious chapter of &#39;Mary Backstayge, Noble Wife&#39;, where Harry Backstayge tries to direct a disastrous summer stock theater production in Skunk Haven, Long Island.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 25</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 25</strong>: 47</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
