    "html": "<h2>August 25 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 67)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, serial multi-part narrative arcs, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 25</strong> across American network, civil rights docudrama, pulp detective, hardboiled adventure serials, and midnight horror radio history (1940–1951):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 25 Archival Inventory (Increment 67)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>ILAM_1943_08_25.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Murder Is Word Part 13</code>, <code>Mountain Lodge Trap</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Murder Is the Word for It: The Mountain Lodge Trap (Part 13 — Carlton E. Morse — Ivory Soap)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WABC)</strong><br>1943-08-25<br>7:00 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Procter &amp; Gamble</strong><br>Ivory Bar Soap &amp; Oxydol</td>\n<td><strong>Michael Raffetto</strong>, <strong>Barton Yarborough</strong>, <strong>M. McCambridge</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Carlton E. Morse</strong>, <strong>Rex Koury</strong>, <strong>Dresser Dahlstead</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Hardboiled&quot;, &quot;Adventure&quot;, &quot;Suspense&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>In Part 13 of <em>Murder Is the Word for It</em>, Jack Packard and Doc Long face a lethal setup in a storm-isolated mountain lodge when a psychotic killer cuts the telephone wires and locks the exits for Ivory Bar Soap.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ILAM_1944_08_25.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Temple Vampires Part 4</code>, <code>Bat Caverns Morse</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Temple of Vampires: The Bat Caverns (Part 4 — Carlton E. Morse — Oxydol)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WABC)</strong><br>1944-08-25<br>7:00 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Procter &amp; Gamble</strong><br>Oxydol — &quot;Hustle-Bubble&quot;</td>\n<td><strong>Michael Raffetto</strong>, <strong>Barton Yarborough</strong>, <strong>Gloria Blondell</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Carlton E. Morse</strong>, <strong>Rex Koury</strong>, <strong>Dresser Dahlstead</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Hardboiled&quot;, &quot;Adventure&quot;, &quot;Occult&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>In Part 4 of <em>The Temple of Vampires</em>, Jack, Doc, and Sunny explore the subterranean ruins of an ancient Mexican pagan pyramid, discovering a terrifying subterranean colony of blood-drinking bats guarding a stolen Mayan jade cache for Oxydol.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ILAM_1950_08_25.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Turquoise Pass Part 5</code>, <code>Snipers Ambush 1950</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Murder in Turquoise Pass: The Sniper&#39;s Ambush (Part 5 — Carlton E. Morse — Mutual)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mutual (WOR)</strong><br>1950-08-25<br>7:45 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Adventure Serial</td>\n<td><strong>Russell Thorson</strong>, <strong>Jim Boles</strong>, <strong>Tony Randall</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Carlton E. Morse</strong>, <strong>Don Dowd</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Hardboiled&quot;, &quot;Adventure&quot;, &quot;Western&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>In Part 5 of <em>Murder in Turquoise Pass</em>, Jack Packard and Doc Long track an armed band of silver-mine claim jumpers through a treacherous Southwestern mountain gorge, dodging sniper fire from a hidden ridge ambush.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Destination_Freedom_1951.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Destination Freedom 1951</code>, <code>Sojourner Truth Long Road</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Story of Sojourner Truth: The Long Road (Richard Durham — Destination Freedom)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WMAQ)</strong><br>1951-08-25<br>10:00 AM CDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Civil Rights Docudrama</td>\n<td><strong>Fred Pinkard</strong>, <strong>Oscar Brown Jr.</strong>, <strong>Janice Kingslow</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Richard Durham</strong> (Writer), <strong>Homer Heck</strong>, <strong>Hugh Downs</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Historical&quot;, &quot;Civil Rights&quot;, &quot;Black History&quot;, &quot;Biographical&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Written by groundbreaking dramatist Richard Durham, <em>Destination Freedom</em> presents the courageous life of abolitionist and women&#39;s rights pioneer Sojourner Truth, chronicling her escape from slavery in New York and her tireless national crusade for racial equality.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Charlie_Chan_1947.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Talking Doll</code>, <code>Charlie Chan Feen a Mint</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Case of the Talking Doll (Ed Begley as Charlie Chan — Feen-a-Mint)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mutual (WOR)</strong><br>1947-08-25<br>8:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Pharmaco Inc.</strong><br>Feen-a-Mint &amp; Chooz</td>\n<td><strong>Ed Begley</strong> (Charlie Chan), <strong>Leon Janney</strong> (Son)</td>\n<td><strong>Earl Derr Biggers</strong>, <strong>Chick Vincent</strong>, <strong>Dorian St. George</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Procedural&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Inspector Charlie Chan speaking...&quot; Renowned Honolulu detective Charlie Chan and his energetic Number One Son investigate the murder of an eccentric toy inventor whose robotic talking doll conceals a hidden cylinder recording naming his killer for Feen-a-Mint.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Hermits_Cave_1940.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Episode ++++</code>, <code>Gallows Tree Hermits Cave</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Ghost of the Gallows Tree (John Kent as The Hermit — Carter&#39;s Pills)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>WJR Detroit</strong><br>1940-08-25<br>10:30 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>Carter Products</strong><br>Little Liver Pills</td>\n<td><strong>John Kent</strong> (The Hermit), <strong>WJR Repertory</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Charles Penman</strong> (Writer/Dir.), <strong>Mel Marshall</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Horror&quot;, &quot;Suspense&quot;, &quot;Macabre&quot;, &quot;Supernatural&quot;, &quot;Audio Drama&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Ghost stories! Weird stories! And murders, too! The Hermit knows of them all...&quot; The cackling Hermit tells the macabre tale of an executioner haunted by the vengeful phantom of an innocent man hanged beneath an ancient gallows oak tree for Carter&#39;s Little Liver Pills.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 25</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 25</strong>: 17</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
