    "html": "<h2>August 25 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 66)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, serial multi-part narrative arcs, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 25</strong> across American network, big band swing, pulp masked hero adventure, Major League Baseball broadcasts, and hardboiled detective mystery radio history (1939–1947):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 25 Archival Inventory (Increment 66)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Melton_1946_08_25.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Guest Jane Wilson</code>, <code>James Melton Texaco 1946</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Guest Lyric Soprano Jane Wilson (James Melton — Texaco)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WCBS)</strong><br>1946-08-25<br>9:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Texas Company</strong><br>Texaco Fire-Chief Gas</td>\n<td><strong>James Melton</strong> (Tenor), <strong>Jane Wilson</strong> (Soprano)</td>\n<td><strong>Al Goodman</strong> (Orchestra), <strong>Frank Gallop</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Classical&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;, &quot;Pop&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Metropolitan Opera tenor James Melton welcomes lyric soprano Jane Wilson to perform romantic operatic duets and Broadway show tunes accompanied by Al Goodman&#39;s Orchestra for Texaco Fire-Chief Gasoline.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Avenger_1942_08_25.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>The Avenger 1942</code>, <code>Secret Steel Syndicate</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Secret of the Steel Syndicate (James Monks as The Avenger — Street &amp; Smith)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Syndicated</strong><br>1942-08-25<br>(Pulp Drama)</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Street &amp; Smith Superhero</td>\n<td><strong>James Monks</strong> (Jim Brandon), <strong>Helen Adamson</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Paul Ernst</strong> (Author), <strong>Kenneth MacGregor</strong>, <strong>Ken Powell</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Adventure&quot;, &quot;Superhero&quot;, &quot;Pulp&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Master criminologist and bio-chemist Jim Brandon adopts the masked persona of The Avenger, using his secret invention—the telepathic &#39;telepath-ray&#39; and invisibility diffuser—to thwart a saboteur ring targeting industrial steel mills.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Glenn_Miller_1942_08_25.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Snippet Unknown Location</code>, <code>Glenn Miller Chesterfield</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Chesterfield Moonlight Serenade: Late-Summer Swing (Glenn Miller — Chesterfield)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WABC)</strong><br>1942-08-25<br>10:00 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Liggett &amp; Myers</strong><br>Chesterfield — &quot;They Satisfy!&quot;</td>\n<td><strong>Glenn Miller Orchestra</strong>, <strong>Ray Eberle</strong>, <strong>Modernaires</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Glenn Miller &amp; Jerry Gray</strong>, <strong>Paul Douglas</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;, &quot;Swing&quot;, &quot;Jazz&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;It&#39;s the Chesterfield Moonlight Serenade!&quot; Bandleader Glenn Miller leads his iconic swing orchestra in a crisp late-summer broadcast featuring the signature harmonies of &#39;Moonlight Serenade&#39; and &#39;Kalamazoo&#39; for Chesterfield Cigarettes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Treasury_Agent_1947.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Treasury Agent 1947</code>, <code>Border Smuggling Ring</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Case of the Border Smuggling Ring (Phillips H. Lord — Knox Manning — ABC)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>ABC (WJZ)</strong><br>1947-08-25<br>8:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Law Enforcement Drama</td>\n<td><strong>Knox Manning</strong> (Narrator), <strong>Joe DeSantis</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Phillips H. Lord</strong>, <strong>Leonard Bass</strong>, <strong>Gene Hamilton</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Procedural&quot;, &quot;Docudrama&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Drawn directly from the secret files of the United States Treasury Department, federal agents track an armed international cartel running illicit contraband across the Mexican border, orchestrating a high-stakes sting operation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Vox_Pop_1941_08_25.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Dodgers vs Cardinals</code>, <code>Vox Pop Hotel Bossert 1941</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Live with the Brooklyn Dodgers &amp; St. Louis Cardinals (Hotel Bossert — Bromo-Seltzer)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WABC)</strong><br>1941-08-25<br>8:00 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>Emerson Drug Co.</strong><br>Bromo-Seltzer</td>\n<td><strong>Parks Johnson</strong>, <strong>Warren Hull</strong>, <strong>MLB Players</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS Special Events</strong>, <strong>Herb Moss</strong>, <strong>Roger Krupp</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Talk&quot;, &quot;Interview&quot;, &quot;Sports&quot;, &quot;Baseball&quot;, &quot;Human Interest&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Vox Pop, the voice of the people!&quot; Co-hosts Parks Johnson and Warren Hull broadcast live from the rooftop of Brooklyn&#39;s historic Hotel Bossert, interviewing star players and managers of the Brooklyn Dodgers and St. Louis Cardinals in the heat of the 1941 National League pennant race for Bromo-Seltzer.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ILAM_1939_08_25.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Incident Death Part 13</code>, <code>Roxbury Terror Part 13</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Roxbury Terror: The Embalming Crypt (Part 13 — Carlton E. Morse — Fleischmann&#39;s)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Red (WEAF)</strong><br>1939-08-25<br>7:15 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>Standard Brands Inc.</strong><br>Fleischmann&#39;s Yeast</td>\n<td><strong>Michael Raffetto</strong>, <strong>Barton Yarborough</strong>, <strong>W. Paterson</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Carlton E. Morse</strong>, <strong>Rex Koury</strong>, <strong>Dresser Dahlstead</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Hardboiled&quot;, &quot;Adventure&quot;, &quot;Suspense&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Jack, Doc, and Reggie... three men who go anywhere, do anything, so long as the trouble is exciting!&quot; In Part 13 of <em>The Roxbury Terror</em>, the A-1 Detective Agency trio uncover a hidden embalming crypt within the Roxbury mansion where an assassin lies in wait for Fleischmann&#39;s Yeast.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 25</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 25</strong>: 11</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
