    "html": "<h2>August 25 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 120)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, serial multi-part narrative arcs, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 25</strong> across American network, two satirical comedy tours-de-force by Bob and Ray (including <em>Mary Backstayge</em> &amp; Barry Campbell), Stan Kenton breaking down progressive jazz innovations on NBC with Maynard Ferguson, and three inspiring personal philosophy essays on Edward R. Murrow&#39;s <em>This I Believe</em> from jurist Dr. Edmond R. Schlesinger, clergyman George Stewart, and banker Thomas C. Boushall (1949–1953):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 25 Archival Inventory (Increment 120)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>This_I_Believe_Schlesinger.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Edmond R Schlesinger</code>, <code>Dr Edmond Schlesinger</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Constitutional Law and Human Dignity (Dr. Edmond R. Schlesinger — Edward R. Murrow)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WCBS)</strong><br>1952-08-25<br>(Network Relay)</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Personal Philosophy</td>\n<td><strong>Edward R. Murrow</strong> (Host), <strong>Dr. Edmond R. Schlesinger</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Raymond Swing</strong> (Producer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Philosophy&quot;, &quot;Human Interest&quot;, &quot;History&quot;, &quot;Inspirational&quot;, &quot;Documentary&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;This I Believe, with Edward R. Murrow!&quot; Austrian jurist and political refugee Dr. Edmond R. Schlesinger recounts fleeing Nazi totalitarianism, sharing his hard-won conviction that the bedrock of human dignity rests upon the rule of constitutional law and the sacred freedom of the individual conscience.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>This_I_Believe_Stewart.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>George Stewart</code>, <code>Rev George Stewart</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Moral Courage in Times of Crisis (George Stewart — Edward R. Murrow This I Believe)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WCBS)</strong><br>1952-08-25<br>(Network Relay)</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Personal Philosophy</td>\n<td><strong>Edward R. Murrow</strong> (Host), <strong>Rev. Dr. George Stewart</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Raymond Swing</strong> (Producer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Philosophy&quot;, &quot;Human Interest&quot;, &quot;Inspirational&quot;, &quot;Documentary&quot;, &quot;Religion&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;This I Believe, hosted by Edward R. Murrow!&quot; Clergyman and decorated combat veteran George Stewart reflects on facing fear on the battlefield, declaring his bedrock faith that quiet moral courage and compassionate self-sacrifice are the true arbiters of a meaningful life.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>This_I_Believe_Boushall.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Thomas C Boushall</code>, <code>Thomas Boushall Banker</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Banking as a Social Trust (Thomas C. Boushall — Edward R. Murrow This I Believe)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WCBS)</strong><br>1952-08-25<br>(Network Relay)</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Personal Philosophy</td>\n<td><strong>Edward R. Murrow</strong> (Host), <strong>Thomas C. Boushall</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Raymond Swing</strong> (Producer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Philosophy&quot;, &quot;Human Interest&quot;, &quot;Inspirational&quot;, &quot;Economics&quot;, &quot;Documentary&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;This I Believe, with Edward R. Murrow!&quot; Innovative Virginia banker Thomas C. Boushall explains his philosophy of banking as a social trust, maintaining that financial enterprise must always serve human welfare by extending credit and opportunity to working-class families.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Stan_Kenton_1953.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Ep059 Behind The Scenes</code>, <code>Behind the Scenes Kenton</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Behind the Scenes: Innovations in Modern Music (Stan Kenton &amp; His Orchestra — NBC)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WNBC)</strong><br>1953-08-25<br>9:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Progressive Jazz Showcase</td>\n<td><strong>Stan Kenton</strong>, <strong>Maynard Ferguson</strong>, <strong>Frank Rosolino</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Jazz Dept.</strong>, <strong>Gene Hamilton</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Jazz&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;, &quot;Progressive Jazz&quot;, &quot;Documentary&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;The Stan Kenton Show, presenting Innovations in Modern Music!&quot; Maestro Stan Kenton takes listeners behind the scenes of his revolutionary 20-piece progressive jazz orchestra, discussing daring modern arrangements with arranger Bill Russo and featuring explosive brass solos from Maynard Ferguson and Frank Rosolino.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Bob_Ray_1949.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Barry Campbell&#39;s Motion Picture</code>, <code>Barry Campbells Motion Picture</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Barry Campbell&#39;s Motion Picture Debut (Bob Elliott &amp; Ray Goulding — Matinee)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>WHDH Boston</strong><br>1949-08-25<br>1:15 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Deadpan Satire</td>\n<td><strong>Bob Elliott</strong>, <strong>Ray Goulding</strong> (Barry Campbell)</td>\n<td><strong>Bob Elliott &amp; Ray Goulding</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Satire&quot;, &quot;Parody&quot;, &quot;Showbiz&quot;, &quot;Skits&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Matinee with Bob and Ray!&quot; Comedy masters Bob Elliott and Ray Goulding present a deadpan interview with pompous Broadway actor Barry Campbell, who describes his utterly disastrous, low-budget Hollywood motion picture debut while spoofing cinematic grandeur.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Bob_Ray_1952.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Episode</code>, <code>Mary Backstayge Bob and Ray</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Mary Backstayge, Noble Wife &amp; Wally Ballou (Bob &amp; Ray — CBS)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WCBS)</strong><br>1952-08-25<br>5:45 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Radio Comedy Satire</td>\n<td><strong>Bob Elliott</strong> (Wally Ballou), <strong>Ray Goulding</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Bob Elliott &amp; Ray Goulding</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Satire&quot;, &quot;Parody&quot;, &quot;Skits&quot;, &quot;Absurdist&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;It&#39;s Bob and Ray on the CBS Radio Network!&quot; Bob Elliott and Ray Goulding present an absurdist parody of radio soap operas with an episode of &#39;Mary Backstayge, Noble Wife&#39;, alongside roving reporter Wally Ballou interviewing a hotel lobby clerk who refuses to check in any guests.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 25</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 25</strong>: 335</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
