    "html": "<h2>August 25 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 116)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, serial multi-part narrative arcs, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 25</strong> across American network, three eras of Bing Crosby (including GE small appliances and duets with Rosemary Clooney), Tommy Dorsey&#39;s big band swing for Lucky Strike cigarettes, the thrilling serial finale of <em>The Twilight Terror</em> with Nick Carter, and inspirational rehabilitation dramas on <em>America on the March</em> for the DAV (1943–1961):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 25 Archival Inventory (Increment 116)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Bing_Crosby_1955.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Episode 199</code>, <code>Bing General Electric 1955</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Learnin&#39; the Blues (Bing Crosby &amp; Buddy Cole Trio — General Electric)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WCBS)</strong><br>1955-08-25<br>9:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>General Electric</strong><br>GE Telechron &amp; Appliances</td>\n<td><strong>Bing Crosby</strong>, <strong>Buddy Cole Trio</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Bill Morrow</strong> (Producer/Dir.), <strong>Ken Carpenter</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;, &quot;Pop&quot;, &quot;Jazz&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;The Bing Crosby Show, brought to you by General Electric!&quot; Der Bingle trades relaxed banter with Ken Carpenter and performs intimate swing arrangements of &#39;Learnin&#39; the Blues&#39; and &#39;Autumn in New York&#39; backed by the Buddy Cole Trio for General Electric.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Bing_Rosie_1960.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>First Song - New Sun In The Sky</code>, <code>New Sun in Sky 1960</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;New Sun in the Sky (Bing Crosby &amp; Rosemary Clooney — CBS)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WCBS)</strong><br>1960-08-25<br>10:45 AM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Morning Music Duets</td>\n<td><strong>Bing Crosby</strong>, <strong>Rosemary Clooney</strong>, <strong>Buddy Cole Trio</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Bill Morrow</strong> (Dir.), <strong>Ken Carpenter</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;, &quot;Duet&quot;, &quot;Pop&quot;, &quot;Jazz&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;The Bing Crosby and Rosemary Clooney Show, presented by CBS!&quot; Bing and Rosie open with an upbeat rendition of Arthur Schwartz&#39;s &#39;New Sun in the Sky&#39;, followed by Rosie&#39;s solo of &#39;Tenderly&#39; and a charming mid-century duet on &#39;Something&#39;s Gotta Give&#39;.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Bing_Rosie_1961.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>First Song - It Happened In Monterey</code>, <code>It Happened in Monterey</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;It Happened in Monterey (Bing Crosby &amp; Rosemary Clooney — CBS)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WCBS)</strong><br>1961-08-25<br>10:45 AM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Morning Music Duets</td>\n<td><strong>Bing Crosby</strong>, <strong>Rosemary Clooney</strong>, <strong>Buddy Cole Trio</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Bill Morrow</strong> (Dir.), <strong>Ken Carpenter</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;, &quot;Duet&quot;, &quot;Pop&quot;, &quot;Jazz&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Bing Crosby and Rosemary Clooney with the Buddy Cole Trio!&quot; Bing serenades listeners with the 1930 waltz favorite &#39;It Happened in Monterey&#39;, after which Rosemary Clooney delivers a swinging vocal performance on &#39;Love Is Here to Stay&#39;.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Tommy_Dorsey_1943.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Tommy Dorsey 1943-08-25</code>, <code>All-Time Hit Parade 1943</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;I&#39;ll Never Smile Again (Tommy Dorsey &amp; Dick Haymes — Lucky Strike)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Red (WEAF)</strong><br>1943-08-25<br>8:30 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>American Tobacco Co.</strong><br>Lucky Strike (&quot;L.S./M.F.T.&quot;)</td>\n<td><strong>Tommy Dorsey</strong>, <strong>Dick Haymes</strong>, <strong>Pied Pipers</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Foote Cone &amp; Belding</strong>, <strong>Lyle Van</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;, &quot;Swing&quot;, &quot;Jazz&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Lucky Strike presents Tommy Dorsey and the All-Time Hit Parade!&quot; Maestro Tommy Dorsey showcases his golden trombone alongside vocal hits from Dick Haymes and The Pied Pipers, featuring &#39;I&#39;ll Never Smile Again&#39; and &#39;Well, Git It!&#39; for Lucky Strike.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>America_March_1955.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>The Story of a Poet</code>, <code>Story of a Blind Poet DAV</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Story of a Poet (Narrator Alan Baxter — Disabled American Veterans)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>DAV Syndication</strong><br>1955-08-25<br>(PR Division)</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>DAV Public Service</td>\n<td><strong>Alan Baxter</strong> (Narrator), <strong>John McIntire</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Disabled American Veterans National PR</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Documentary&quot;, &quot;Biography&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Public Service&quot;, &quot;Military&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;America on the March, presented in the public interest by the Disabled American Veterans!&quot; A moving biographical dramatization recounting the inspiring journey of a blinded combat veteran who overcomes physical trauma through the power of poetry and vocational rehabilitation to become a published author.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Nick_Carter_1944.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>The Twilight Terror, Part 5</code>, <code>Twilight Terror Part 5</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Twilight Terror (Part 5 — Lon Clark as Nick Carter — Mutual)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mutual (WOR)</strong><br>1944-08-25<br>8:00 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Detective Serial</td>\n<td><strong>Lon Clark</strong> (Nick Carter), <strong>Charlotte Manson</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Jock MacGregor</strong>, <strong>Michael Fitzmaurice</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Hardboiled&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Nick Carter, Master Detective, based on the famous Street &amp; Smith stories!&quot; In Part 5 (Conclusion) of <em>The Twilight Terror</em>, Nick Carter and Patsy Bowen track the mysterious cloaked figure stalking the seaside mansion to a secret subterranean smugglers&#39; tunnel, unmasking the criminal mastermind before he can detonate the cliffside estate.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 25</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 25</strong>: 311</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
