    "html": "<h2>August 25 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 104)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, serial multi-part narrative arcs, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 25</strong> across American network, three poignant wartime and pre-war episodes of Gertrude Berg&#39;s <em>The Goldbergs</em> for Duz Soap, courtroom drama on <em>Perry Mason</em> starring John Larkin for Tide, Gordon MacRae &amp; Evelyn Knight on <em>The Texaco Star Theater</em>, and U.S. Air Force big band concerts on <em>Serenade in Blue</em> (1941–1959):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 25 Archival Inventory (Increment 104)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Serenade_Blue_1959.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>First Tune Is I Want To Be Happy</code>, <code>Air Force Band 1959</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;I Want to Be Happy (Col. George S. Howard — U.S. Air Force)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>USAF Synd.</strong><br>1959-08-25<br>(Bolling AFB)</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Air Force Recruiting</td>\n<td><strong>Col. George S. Howard</strong>, <strong>Singing Sergeants</strong></td>\n<td><strong>USAF Recruiting Service</strong>, <strong>Bill Steele</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;, &quot;Swing&quot;, &quot;Military&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Serenade in Blue, presented by the United States Air Force!&quot; Col. George S. Howard conducts the elite USAF Symphony Orchestra and The Singing Sergeants in dazzling big band arrangements, opening with a swinging rendition of &#39;I Want to Be Happy&#39; alongside lush vocal harmonies.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Perry_Mason_1954.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Ed Beekman&#39;s Daughter</code>, <code>Perry Mason Tide 1954</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Case of Ed Beekman&#39;s Daughter (John Larkin as Perry Mason — Tide)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WCBS)</strong><br>1954-08-25<br>2:15 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Procter &amp; Gamble</strong><br>Tide Laundry Detergent</td>\n<td><strong>John Larkin</strong> (Perry), <strong>Joan Alexander</strong> (Della)</td>\n<td><strong>Erle Stanley Gardner</strong>, <strong>Arthur Hanna</strong>, <strong>Bob Dixon</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Courtroom&quot;, &quot;Legal&quot;, &quot;Soap Opera&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Perry Mason, defender of the defenseless, brought to you by Tide!&quot; Defense attorney Perry Mason and confidential secretary Della Street cross-examine a reluctant key witness when client Ed Beekman&#39;s estranged daughter is framed for industrial theft for Tide.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Goldbergs_1941.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>The Goldbergs 1941-08-25</code>, <code>Sammys Pre-Law Dilemma</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Sammy&#39;s Pre-Law Dilemma (Gertrude Berg as Molly Goldberg — Duz)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WABC)</strong><br>1941-08-25<br>5:00 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>Procter &amp; Gamble</strong><br>Duz — &quot;Does Everything!&quot;</td>\n<td><strong>Gertrude Berg</strong> (Molly), <strong>James R. Waters</strong> (Jake)</td>\n<td><strong>Gertrude Berg</strong> (Writer), <strong>Bud Collyer</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Domestic&quot;, &quot;Family&quot;, &quot;Jewish&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Yoo-hoo, Mrs. Bloom! It&#39;s The Goldbergs, brought to you by Duz!&quot; Molly Goldberg leans out the tenement window to dispense warm maternal advice to neighbors, before intervening to soothe Jake&#39;s anxieties over son Sammy&#39;s upcoming pre-law examinations for Duz Soap.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Goldbergs_1942.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Emma Can Stay</code>, <code>The Goldbergs Duz 1942</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Emma Can Stay for Nurse&#39;s Training (Gertrude Berg — Duz)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WABC)</strong><br>1942-08-25<br>5:00 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Procter &amp; Gamble</strong><br>Duz — &quot;Does Everything!&quot;</td>\n<td><strong>Gertrude Berg</strong> (Molly), <strong>James R. Waters</strong> (Jake)</td>\n<td><strong>Gertrude Berg</strong> (Writer), <strong>Bud Collyer</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Domestic&quot;, &quot;Family&quot;, &quot;Jewish&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Duz does everything! Here are The Goldbergs!&quot; Molly convinces skeptical husband Jake that their distant cousin Emma must be allowed to extend her summer stay in the Bronx apartment so she can complete her wartime nurse&#39;s training for Duz.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Goldbergs_1943.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>The Secret Is Out</code>, <code>Rosalies Wartime Romance</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Rosalie&#39;s Wartime Romance Secret (Gertrude Berg — Duz)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WABC)</strong><br>1943-08-25<br>1:45 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Procter &amp; Gamble</strong><br>Duz — &quot;Does Everything!&quot;</td>\n<td><strong>Gertrude Berg</strong> (Molly), <strong>James R. Waters</strong> (Jake)</td>\n<td><strong>Gertrude Berg</strong> (Writer), <strong>Bud Collyer</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Domestic&quot;, &quot;Family&quot;, &quot;Jewish&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;The Goldbergs, written and created by Gertrude Berg, brought to you by Duz!&quot; Molly discovers Rosalie&#39;s secret correspondence with an overseas soldier, stepping in with characteristic warmth and humor to prevent Jake from overreacting for Duz.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Texaco_Star_Theater_1948.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>First Song - You Call Everybody Darling</code>, <code>Gordon MacRae Texaco 1948</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;You Call Everybody Darling (Gordon MacRae &amp; Evelyn Knight — Texaco)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WCBS)</strong><br>1948-08-25<br>9:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>The Texas Co.</strong><br>Texaco Fire-Chief Gas</td>\n<td><strong>Gordon MacRae</strong>, <strong>Evelyn Knight</strong>, <strong>Modernaires</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Victor Young Orchestra</strong>, <strong>Jimmy Wallington</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Pop&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;The Texaco Star Theater, starring Gordon MacRae, brought to you by your friendly Texaco Dealer!&quot; Golden-voiced baritone Gordon MacRae and sultry songstress Evelyn Knight perform top Billboard hits of the summer including &#39;You Call Everybody Darling&#39; and &#39;Buttons and Bows&#39; with Victor Young&#39;s Orchestra for Texaco.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 25</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 25</strong>: 239</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
