    "html": "<h2>August 24 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 61)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, serial multi-part narrative arcs, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 24</strong> across modern Holmesian audio drama, swing big bands, 1950s Chicago DJ broadcasts, and BBC sketch satire radio history (1941–2008):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 24 Archival Inventory (Increment 61)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Holmes_2003_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Great American Holmes</code>, <code>Crisis Appearance Evil</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Adventure of the Great American &amp; The Appearance of Evil (Jim French — Imagination Theater)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Syndicated</strong><br>2003-08-24<br>8:00 PM PDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Audio Drama Series</td>\n<td><strong>John Patrick Lowrie</strong> (Holmes), <strong>Lawrence Albert</strong> (Watson)</td>\n<td><strong>M.J. Elliott</strong>, <strong>Jim French</strong>, <strong>Eric Aho</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Victorian&quot;, &quot;Suspense&quot;, &quot;Audio Drama&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Master detective Sherlock Holmes and Dr. Watson investigate the sudden disappearance of a wealthy American railroad tycoon visiting London in &#39;The Adventure of the Great American&#39; (Episode 40), paired with the psychological thriller &#39;The Appearance of Evil&#39; on Jim French&#39;s Imagination Theater.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Lucky_Jack_2008.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Lucky Jack and Helmet</code>, <code>Imagination Theater 2008</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Lucky Jack &amp; The Helmet (Starring John Gilbert &amp; Larry Albert — Imagination Theater)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Syndicated</strong><br>2008-08-24<br>8:00 PM PDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Audio Drama Series</td>\n<td><strong>John Gilbert</strong>, <strong>Larry Albert</strong>, <strong>Dennis Bateman</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Jim French &amp; Matthew Elliott</strong> (Writers)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Adventure&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Fantasy&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;, &quot;Audio Drama&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>A thrilling audio drama double bill featuring soldier of fortune Lucky Jack in an exotic treasure hunt across the Mediterranean, paired with &#39;The Helmet,&#39; a supernatural mystery regarding an ancient enchanted warrior&#39;s helmet.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Fitch_1941_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Jan Savitt Top Hatters</code>, <code>Fitch Shampoo 1941</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Hop Aboard the Bandwagon: Jan Savitt &amp; His Top Hatters (Fitch Shampoo)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Red (WEAF)</strong><br>1941-08-24<br>7:30 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>F.W. Fitch Co.</strong><br>Dandruff Remover Shampoo</td>\n<td><strong>Jan Savitt and his Top Hatters</strong>, <strong>Allan DeWitt</strong></td>\n<td><strong>L.W. Ramsey Co.</strong>, <strong>Homer Fickett</strong>, <strong>Toby David</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;, &quot;Swing&quot;, &quot;Jazz&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Hop aboard the Fitch Bandwagon!&quot; Jan Savitt and his Top Hatters jump into high-gear swing rhythm with &#39;720 in the Books&#39;, &#39;It&#39;s a Wonderful World&#39;, and hot shuffle beat solos for Fitch&#39;s Dandruff Remover Shampoo.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Howard_Miller_1955_A.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Dick Kilner Part 1</code>, <code>Howard Miller WGN 1955</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Guest Record Promoter Dick Kilner (Part 1 — Howard Miller — WGN)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS / WGN</strong><br>1955-08-24<br>10:45 AM CDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>WGN Chicago Broadcast</td>\n<td><strong>Howard Miller</strong> (DJ Host), <strong>Dick Kilner</strong> (Promoter)</td>\n<td><strong>WGN Wrigley Building Studios Chicago</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Talk&quot;, &quot;Interview&quot;, &quot;Disk Jockey&quot;, &quot;Pop&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Chicago&#39;s king of morning disk jockeys Howard Miller spins the latest Billboard chart-toppers and interviews music promoter Dick Kilner about breakthrough rhythm and blues and pop vocal releases.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Howard_Miller_1955_B.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Dick Kilner Part 2</code>, <code>Howard Miller Midwest Jazz</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Midwest Jazz Tours with Dick Kilner (Part 2 — Howard Miller — WGN)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS / WGN</strong><br>1955-08-24<br>11:00 AM CDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>WGN Chicago Broadcast</td>\n<td><strong>Howard Miller</strong> (Host), <strong>Dick Kilner</strong> (Guest)</td>\n<td><strong>WGN Chicago Broadcast Team</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Talk&quot;, &quot;Interview&quot;, &quot;Disk Jockey&quot;, &quot;Pop&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>In the second segment of the morning broadcast, Howard Miller and guest Dick Kilner discuss upcoming jazz concert tours in the Midwest, playing fresh singles from Capitol and Columbia Records.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ISIRTA_1975_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Audible Road Signs</code>, <code>Im Sorry Ill Read That Again</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Audible Road Signs (The Goodies &amp; John Cleese — BBC Radio 2)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Radio 2</strong><br>1975-08-24<br>2:00 PM BST</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>BBC Comedy Special</td>\n<td><strong>John Cleese</strong>, <strong>Tim Brooke-Taylor</strong>, <strong>Graeme Garden</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Graeme Garden &amp; Bill Oddie</strong>, <strong>Humphrey Barclay</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Satire&quot;, &quot;Sketch&quot;, &quot;British&quot;, &quot;Parody&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>The Cambridge Footlights comedy gang unleash an avalanche of rapid-fire puns, sound-effect gags, and surreal sketches about government-mandated &#39;talking road signs&#39; that shout unsolicited driving criticisms at passing motorists.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 24</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 24</strong>: 366</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
