    "html": "<h2>August 24 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 59)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, serial multi-part narrative arcs, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 24</strong> across American network, variety galas, psychological crime deduction, and supernatural horror radio history (1935–1954):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 24 Archival Inventory (Increment 59)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Shell_Chateau_1935_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Shell Chateau Al Jolson</code>, <code>Judy Garland Debut 1935</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Judy Garland&#39;s Singing Debut (Al Jolson — Shell Chateau)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Red (WEAF)</strong><br>1935-08-24<br>9:30 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>Shell Oil Company</strong><br>Shell Super-Charged Gas</td>\n<td><strong>Al Jolson</strong> (Host), <strong>Judy Garland</strong> (13-Year-Old Debut)</td>\n<td><strong>J. Walter Thompson</strong>, <strong>Bill Bacher</strong>, <strong>Jimmy Wallington</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;, &quot;Vaudeville&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>World-famous entertainer Al Jolson hosts a star-studded gala at the <em>Shell Chateau</em>, introducing a 13-year-old singing sensation named Judy Garland in a breathtaking vocal debut alongside Victor Young&#39;s Orchestra for Shell Super-Charged Gasoline.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Crime_Doctor_1941_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Forged Parole</code>, <code>Dr Benjamin Ordway 1941</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Case of the Forged Parole (Ray Collins as Dr. Ordway — Philip Morris)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WABC)</strong><br>1941-08-24<br>8:30 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>Philip Morris &amp; Co.</strong><br>Philip Morris Cigarettes</td>\n<td><strong>Ray Collins</strong> (Dr. Ordway), <strong>Everett Sloane</strong>, <strong>Santos Ortega</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Max Marcin</strong> (Creator), <strong>Jack Johnstone</strong>, <strong>Charles O&#39;Connor</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Medical&quot;, &quot;Psychological&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Former criminal turned forensic psychiatrist Dr. Benjamin Ordway uses psychological profiling to investigate a forged prison parole petition, exposing a corrupt attorney orchestrating an armed jewel heist ring for Philip Morris.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Crime_Doctor_1947_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Blind Witness</code>, <code>Dr Benjamin Ordway 1947</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Case of the Blind Witness (John McIntire as Dr. Ordway — Philip Morris)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WCBS)</strong><br>1947-08-24<br>8:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Philip Morris &amp; Co.</strong><br>Philip Morris Cigarettes</td>\n<td><strong>John McIntire</strong> (Dr. Ordway), <strong>Virginia Gregg</strong>, <strong>William Conrad</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Max Marcin</strong> (Creator), <strong>Jack Johnstone</strong>, <strong>Ken Roberts</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Medical&quot;, &quot;Psychological&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Dr. Benjamin Ordway interrogates an apparently blind eyewitness whose sensory recollections of a suburban murder scene contain psychological inconsistencies that point to premeditated perjury.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Barry_Craig_1954_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Blood Money</code>, <code>Barry Craig Confidential 1954</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Blood Money (William Gargan as Barry Craig — NBC)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WNBC)</strong><br>1954-08-24<br>9:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Detective Noir Series</td>\n<td><strong>William Gargan</strong> (Barry Craig), <strong>Ralph Bell</strong>, <strong>Joan Lazer</strong></td>\n<td><strong>John Roeburt</strong> (Writer), <strong>Himan Brown</strong>, <strong>Don Pardo</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Hardboiled&quot;, &quot;Noir&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;My name is Barry Craig, confidential investigator...&quot; Barry Craig is hired by an anxious heiress to deliver a suitcase containing fifty thousand dollars in ransom payoff money, only to uncover that the kidnapping was staged by the girl&#39;s degenerate gambler boyfriend.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Quiet_Please_1947_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Mirror Mirror on the Wall</code>, <code>Quiet Please Wyllis Cooper</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Mirror, Mirror on the Wall (Wyllis Cooper — Ernest Chappell — Quiet, Please)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mutual (WOR)</strong><br>1947-08-24<br>3:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Horror Anthology</td>\n<td><strong>Ernest Chappell</strong> (Narrator / Lead), <strong>Claudia Morgan</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Wyllis Cooper</strong> (Writer/Dir.), <strong>Gene Perazzo</strong> (Organ)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Horror&quot;, &quot;Suspense&quot;, &quot;Psychological&quot;, &quot;Supernatural&quot;, &quot;Audio Drama&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Quiet, please... quietly, if you will...&quot; A chilling tale written by Wyllis Cooper about a vain antique collector who purchases an ornate 16th-century Venetian looking-glass that gradually begins reflecting a sinister, malevolent entity living inside the glass instead of his own face.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Saturday_Night_1946_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>The Farmers Wife</code>, <code>Saturday Night Theatre 1946</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Farmer&#39;s Wife (Eden Phillpotts — Frank Pettingell — BBC)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Home</strong><br>1946-08-24<br>9:15 PM BST</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>BBC Regional Drama</td>\n<td><strong>Frank Pettingell</strong>, <strong>Gladys Young</strong>, <strong>Carleton Hobbs</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Eden Phillpotts</strong>, <strong>Hugh Stewart</strong>, <strong>Val Gielgud</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;British&quot;, &quot;Regional&quot;, &quot;Romance&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>In Eden Phillpotts&#39; beloved Devonshire rural romantic comedy, middle-aged widower Samuel Sweetland draws up a shortlist of eligible local spinsters to wed, blissfully unaware that true love and devotion have been quietly managing his household all along in the person of his loyal housekeeper Araminta.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 24</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 24</strong>: 354</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
