    "html": "<h2>August 24 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 55)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, serial multi-part narrative arcs, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 24</strong> across American network, Broadway war drama, and vocal countdown radio history (1946–1986):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 24 Archival Inventory (Increment 55)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Hit_Parade_1946_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Prisoner Of Love</code>, <code>Frank Sinatra Hit Parade 1946</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Prisoner of Love (Frank Sinatra &amp; Joan Edwards — Lucky Strike)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WEAF)</strong><br>1946-08-24<br>9:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>American Tobacco Co.</strong><br>Lucky Strike — &quot;L.S./M.F.T.&quot;</td>\n<td><strong>Frank Sinatra</strong>, <strong>Joan Edwards</strong>, <strong>Pied Pipers</strong>, <strong>Mark Warnow</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Lord &amp; Thomas</strong>, <strong>Axel Stordahl</strong>, <strong>Andre Baruch</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Countdown&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;, &quot;Pop&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;The lucky strike hit parade!&quot; Frank Sinatra croons the nation&#39;s number-one hit ballad &#39;Prisoner of Love&#39; along with &#39;Surrender&#39; and &#39;They Say It&#39;s Wonderful&#39; accompanied by Mark Warnow&#39;s Orchestra for Lucky Strike Cigarettes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Maisie_1950_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>The Orphanage</code>, <code>Ann Sothern Maisie 1950</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Orphanage Benefit (Starring Ann Sothern as Maisie Ravier — Mutual)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mutual (WOR)</strong><br>1950-08-24<br>8:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>MGM Radio Attractions</td>\n<td><strong>Ann Sothern</strong> (Maisie), <strong>Hans Conried</strong>, <strong>Elvia Allman</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Arthur Phillips</strong> (Writer), <strong>John L. Greene</strong>, <strong>John Heistand</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Sitcom&quot;, &quot;Domestic&quot;, &quot;Hollywood&quot;, &quot;Vaudeville&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Brooklyn showgirl Maisie Ravier volunteers at a local children&#39;s orphanage to help stage a summer charity benefit, but gets caught in a hilarious tug-of-war with a strict board director to find an adoptive home for a mischievous stray youngster.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Best_Plays_1952_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Home Of The Brave</code>, <code>John Cassavetes Best Plays</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Home of the Brave (Starring John Cassavetes — Arthur Laurents — NBC)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WNBC)</strong><br>1952-08-24<br>6:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>NBC Broadway Theater</td>\n<td><strong>John Cassavetes</strong> (Peter Coen), <strong>Frank Overton</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Arthur Laurents</strong> (Playwright), <strong>John Chapman</strong>, <strong>Ben Grauer</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Military&quot;, &quot;Psychological&quot;, &quot;World War II&quot;, &quot;Broadway&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Drama critic John Chapman presents Arthur Laurents&#39; powerful Broadway war drama <em>Home of the Brave</em>, exploring battlefield trauma and anti-Semitism through the psychological rehabilitation of a paralyzed young Jewish infantry soldier (John Cassavetes) on a remote South Pacific island.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Sexton_Blake_1967_08_24_A.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Lillies For The Ladies</code>, <code>Sexton Blake BBC 1967</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Lilies for the Ladies (Part 5 — William Franklyn as Sexton Blake — BBC)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Radio 4</strong><br>1967-08-24<br>7:30 PM BST</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>BBC Crime Serial</td>\n<td><strong>William Franklyn</strong> (Sexton Blake), <strong>David Gregory</strong> (Tinker)</td>\n<td><strong>Donald Stuart</strong> (Writer), <strong>Joe Burroughs</strong> (Producer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Procedural&quot;, &quot;British&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Baker Street detective Sexton Blake and young assistant Tinker track a lethal black-market extortionist leaving white calla lilies beside murdered society heiresses in London&#39;s West End.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Sexton_Blake_1967_08_24_B.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Detective Lillies Ladies</code>, <code>Sexton Blake Final Trap</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Lilies for the Ladies: The Final Trap (Part 5 — William Franklyn — BBC)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Radio 4</strong><br>1967-08-24<br>7:30 PM BST</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>BBC Crime Serial</td>\n<td><strong>William Franklyn</strong> (Blake), <strong>David Gregory</strong>, <strong>John Moffatt</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Donald Stuart</strong> (Writer), <strong>Joe Burroughs</strong> (Producer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Procedural&quot;, &quot;British&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Sexton Blake corners the deadly flower-shop syndicate responsible for sending poisoned calla lilies to Mayfair mansions, solving the final riddle before the killer strikes again.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Hollywood_Theater_1986.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Whole Towns Talking</code>, <code>Edward G Robinson MacGregor</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Whole Town&#39;s Talking (Starring Edward G. Robinson — C.P. MacGregor)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Syndicated</strong><br>1986-08-24<br>(Retrospective)</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Radio Anthology Series</td>\n<td><strong>Edward G. Robinson</strong>, <strong>Lurene Tuttle</strong>, <strong>Herb Vigran</strong></td>\n<td><strong>C.P. MacGregor</strong> (Producer), <strong>Paul Pierce</strong>, <strong>Frank Goss</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;, &quot;Hollywood&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Edward G. Robinson stars as a mild-mannered office clerk who discovers he is the exact physical double of America&#39;s public enemy number one, leading to a hilarious and suspenseful case of mistaken identity with the police and the underworld.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 24</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 24</strong>: 330</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
