    "html": "<h2>August 24 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 50)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, serial multi-part narrative arcs, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 24</strong> across American network, baseball history, biographical docudrama, and classic comedy radio history (1936–1949):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 24 Archival Inventory (Increment 50)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Cavalcade_1942_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Giant In Meadow</code>, <code>Walter Huston Peter Cooper</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Giant in the Meadow: Peter Cooper (Starring Walter Huston — DuPont)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Red (WEAF)</strong><br>1942-08-24<br>8:00 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>E.I. du Pont de Nemours</strong><br>Better Living Chemistry</td>\n<td><strong>Walter Huston</strong> (Peter Cooper), <strong>Jeanette Nolan</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Robert Tallman</strong> (Writer), <strong>Homer Fickett</strong>, <strong>Clayton Collyer</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Historical&quot;, &quot;Biography&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Americana&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Academy Award winner Walter Huston stars as industrialist and philanthropist Peter Cooper, chronicling his invention of the &#39;Tom Thumb&#39; steam locomotive and the construction of Cooper Union to provide free higher education to working-class Americans for DuPont.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Bill_Stern_1945_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Guest Ty Cobb</code>, <code>Georgia Peach Bill Stern</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Georgia Peach: Interview with Ty Cobb (Bill Stern — Colgate)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Red (WEAF)</strong><br>1945-08-24<br>10:30 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Colgate-Palmolive-Peet</strong><br>Colgate Shave &amp; Halo</td>\n<td><strong>Bill Stern</strong> (Host), <strong>Ty Cobb</strong> (Baseball Legend)</td>\n<td><strong>Ted Collins</strong>, <strong>Joseph Mansfield</strong>, <strong>Jimmy Wallington</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Sports&quot;, &quot;Talk&quot;, &quot;Interview&quot;, &quot;Baseball&quot;, &quot;Americana&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Legendary sports commentator Bill Stern welcomes baseball hall-of-famer Ty Cobb (&quot;The Georgia Peach&quot;) for an unforgettable interview recounting his record-setting 4,189 career hits, fierce rivalries with Babe Ruth, and classic Detroit Tigers pennant races for Colgate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Five_Mysteries_1947_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Five Whodunits</code>, <code>Inspector Hearthstone 1947</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Five Whodunits: Inspector Hearthstone&#39;s Cases (John Griggs — Mutual)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mutual (WOR)</strong><br>1947-08-24<br>5:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Interactive Mystery</td>\n<td><strong>John Griggs</strong> (Inspector Hearthstone), <strong>Mandel Kramer</strong></td>\n<td><strong>George Lowther</strong> (Writer), <strong>Leonard Bass</strong>, <strong>Carl Caruso</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Interactive&quot;, &quot;Quiz&quot;, &quot;Whodunit&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Inspector Hearthstone presents five distinct mini-mysteries featuring cryptic clue deductions, challenging armchair radio sleuths at home to identify the culprit in each case before the final commercial reveal.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Twenty_Questions_1946_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Benny Leonard guest</code>, <code>Twenty Questions Ronson 1946</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Animal, Vegetable, or Mineral? (Guest Benny Leonard — Twenty Questions — Ronson)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mutual (WOR)</strong><br>1946-08-24<br>8:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Ronson Art Metal</strong><br>Ronson Pocket Lighters</td>\n<td><strong>Bill Slater</strong> (Host), <strong>Benny Leonard</strong> (Guest), <strong>Fred Van Deventer</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Gary Stevens</strong> (Producer), <strong>Del Sharbutt</strong>, <strong>Frank Waldecker</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Quiz&quot;, &quot;Game Show&quot;, &quot;Panel&quot;, &quot;Sports&quot;, &quot;Comedy&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>&quot;Is it Animal, Vegetable, or Mineral?&quot; Quizmaster Bill Slater and the regular panel welcome boxing legend Benny Leonard to identify mysterious subjects in twenty questions or less for Ronson Pocket Lighters.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Airport_1949_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Prince For A Day</code>, <code>International Airport 1949</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Prince for a Day (Starring Raymond Burr &amp; Jack Moyles — International Airport)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>ABC (WJZ)</strong><br>1949-08-24<br>9:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Aviation Thriller</td>\n<td><strong>Raymond Burr</strong>, <strong>Jack Moyles</strong>, <strong>Lurene Tuttle</strong></td>\n<td><strong>William Froug</strong> (Writer), <strong>Bill Rousseau</strong>, <strong>Don Lowe</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Adventure&quot;, &quot;Aviation&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Tension mounts at a bustling international airport terminal when a visiting Middle Eastern prince escapes royal bodyguards and disappears into the crowds, drawing airport police and federal agents into a high-stakes search before an international flight departs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Fibber_McGee_1936_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Solving Murder Mystery</code>, <code>Fibber McGee Johnsons 1936</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Solving the Murder Mystery (Jim &amp; Marian Jordan — Johnson&#39;s Wax)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Red (WEAF)</strong><br>1936-08-24<br>8:00 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>S.C. Johnson &amp; Son</strong><br>Glo-Coat &amp; Auto Wax</td>\n<td><strong>Jim Jordan</strong> (Fibber), <strong>Marian Jordan</strong> (Molly), <strong>Bill Thompson</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Don Quinn</strong> (Writer), <strong>Cecil Underwood</strong>, <strong>Harlow Wilcox</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Sitcom&quot;, &quot;Domestic&quot;, &quot;Americana&quot;, &quot;Vaudeville&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Fibber McGee becomes convinced he has the forensic genius of Sherlock Holmes after reading a dime-store detective novel, leading Molly and the Wistful Vista neighbors on an absurd amateur sleuthing investigation for Johnson&#39;s Wax.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 24</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 24</strong>: 300</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
