    "html": "<h2>August 24 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 07)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 24</strong> across American network, Hawaiian remote, and AFRS military broadcasting history (1935–1959):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 24 Archival Inventory (Increment 07)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>One_Night_Stand_1944_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>You Gave Me Runaround</code>, <code>Sonny Dunham Hotel New Yorker</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;One Night Stand #352: Sonny Dunham from Hotel New Yorker (AFRS)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>AFRS Unit</strong><br>1944-08-24<br>(Show #352)</td>\n<td><strong>U.S. War Dept</strong><br>Armed Forces Radio Service</td>\n<td><strong>Sonny Dunham</strong> (Bandleader), <strong>Pete Hanley</strong>, <strong>Dorothy Claire</strong></td>\n<td><strong>AFRS Hollywood Unit</strong>, <strong>Dick Joy</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;, &quot;Swing&quot;, &quot;Jazz&quot;, &quot;Morale&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Virtuoso trumpet and trombone bandleader Sonny Dunham broadcasts live from the Terrace Room of the Hotel New Yorker for Allied troops overseas, featuring swinging arrangements of &#39;Memories of You&#39; and &#39;You Gave Me the Runaround&#39;.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Hawaii_Calls_1957_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Real Big Island</code>, <code>Hawaii Calls Waikiki 1957</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Hawaii Calls: A Real Big Island (Live from Waikiki Beach)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>KGU / Mutual</strong><br>1957-08-24<br>9:00 PM HST</td>\n<td><strong>Hawaii Visitors Bureau</strong><br>Pineapple Growers</td>\n<td><strong>Webley Edwards</strong> (Host), <strong>Al Kealoha Perry</strong>, <strong>Haunani Kahalewai</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Webley Edwards</strong> (Producer/Host)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Hawaiian&quot;, &quot;Polynesian&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;, &quot;Easy Listening&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>With the sound of the Pacific surf crashing beneath the giant banyan tree at the Moana Hotel, Webley Edwards presents authentic Hawaiian melodies, slack-key guitars, and songs celebrating the Big Island of Hawaii.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Hawaii_Calls_1959_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Blue Hawaiian Moonlight</code>, <code>Alfred Apaka Statehood</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Hawaii Calls: Blue Hawaiian Moonlight — Statehood Celebration (Alfred Apaka)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>KGU / Mutual</strong><br>1959-08-24<br>9:00 PM HST</td>\n<td><strong>Hawaii Visitors Bureau</strong><br>Matson Lines Statehood</td>\n<td><strong>Webley Edwards</strong> (Host), <strong>Alfred Apaka</strong>, <strong>Al Kealoha Perry</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Webley Edwards</strong> (Producer/Host)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Hawaiian&quot;, &quot;Polynesian&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;, &quot;Easy Listening&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Broadcaster Webley Edwards hosts a celebratory broadcast during Hawaii&#39;s historic admission as the 50th U.S. State, featuring the golden voice of Alfred Apaka serenading listeners with &#39;Blue Hawaiian Moonlight&#39; from the shores of Waikiki.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Night_Cap_1939_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Sea Strikes Back</code>, <code>Frank Hemingway Maritime</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Sea Strikes Back (Frank Hemingway — Night Cap Yarns)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Don Lee / Mutual</strong><br>1939-08-24<br>10:45 PM PST</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Don Lee Storytelling</td>\n<td><strong>Frank Hemingway</strong> (Storyteller), <strong>Milton Charles</strong> (Organist)</td>\n<td><strong>Frank Hemingway</strong> (Author), <strong>Thomas Freebairn-Smith</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Storytelling&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Maritime&quot;, &quot;Adventure&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Frank Hemingway recounts a dramatic maritime yarn about a ruthless clipper ship captain whose greed blinds him to an approaching typhoon off Cape Horn, facing the wrath of the sea when his crew mutinies to save the ship.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>G_Men_1935_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Bremer Kidnapping</code>, <code>G-Men Phillips H Lord</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Edward Bremer Kidnapping Case (G-Men / Gang Busters — Palmolive)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Red (WEAF)</strong><br>1935-08-24<br>8:00 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>Colgate-Palmolive</strong><br>Palmolive Shave Cream</td>\n<td><strong>Phillips H. Lord</strong> (Narrator), <strong>Dwight Weist</strong>, <strong>Arthur Vinton</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Phillips H. Lord</strong> (Creator/Director), <strong>Alois Havrilla</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;True Crime&quot;, &quot;Police&quot;, &quot;Procedural&quot;, &quot;Docudrama&quot;, &quot;FBI&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>In Phillips H. Lord&#39;s hard-hitting FBI docudrama, federal agents hunt down the notorious Barker-Karpis gang following the brutal high-profile kidnapping of wealthy St. Paul banker Edward Bremer.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Gang_Busters_1938_08_24.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Brest &amp; Logan Case</code>, <code>Schwarzkopf Gang Busters</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Brest &amp; Logan Case (Col. H. Norman Schwarzkopf — Gang Busters)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WABC)</strong><br>1938-08-24<br>8:00 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>Colgate-Palmolive</strong><br>Palmolive Shave Cream</td>\n<td><strong>Col. H. Norman Schwarzkopf</strong> (Host), <strong>Frank Readick</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Phillips H. Lord</strong> (Producer), <strong>Frank Gallop</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;True Crime&quot;, &quot;Police&quot;, &quot;Procedural&quot;, &quot;Docudrama&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>With sirens wailing and machine guns rattling, Col. H. Norman Schwarzkopf narrates the gripping police manhunt for Harold Brest and Joseph Logan, a pair of armed prison escapees running an interstate armed robbery spree.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 24</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 24</strong>: 42</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
