    "html": "<h2>August 23 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 62)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 23</strong> across American network, syndication, and modern audio drama history (1942–2009):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 23 Archival Inventory (Increment 62)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Crisis_1998_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Crisis Pigeon Siren</code>, <code>Jim French Double Feature</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Crisis: The Pigeon &amp; Siren in the Night (Jim French Productions)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>KIXI / Syndication</strong><br>1998-08-23<br>(Imagination Theater)</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Syndicated Radio Drama</td>\n<td><strong>John Armstrong</strong>, <strong>Pat French</strong>, <strong>Terry Rose</strong>, <strong>Jim French</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Jim French</strong> (Writer/Director), <strong>Larry Albert</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;, &quot;Suspense&quot;, &quot;Thriller&quot;, &quot;Modern&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>A thrilling double feature of Jim French&#39;s &#39;Crisis&#39; anthologies: in <em>The Pigeon</em>, a timid bank employee is coerced into acting as an inside courier for kidnappers, followed by <em>Siren in the Night</em>, where a coastal lighthouse keeper receives a desperate SOS.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Imagination_2009_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Anna Tisdale January Cove</code>, <code>Jim French 2009</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Impropriety of Anna Tisdale &amp; January Cove (Imagination Theater)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>KIXI / Syndication</strong><br>2009-08-23<br>(Imagination Theater)</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Syndicated Radio Drama</td>\n<td><strong>Larry Albert</strong>, <strong>Jay Green</strong>, <strong>Cynthia Geary</strong>, <strong>David Selby</strong></td>\n<td><strong>M.J. Elliott &amp; Jim French</strong>, <strong>Jim French</strong> (Director)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Romance&quot;, &quot;Modern&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>A double bill of dramatic mysteries: in <em>The Impropriety of Anna Tisdale</em>, a proper Victorian widow&#39;s scandalous secret inheritance threatens to ruin a prominent family, paired with <em>January Cove</em>, where a storm-battered Maine coastal village harbors a deadly fugitive.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Martin_Lewis_1949_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Billie Burke</code>, <code>Martin and Lewis Chesterfield</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Martin and Lewis Show with Guest Billie Burke (Chesterfield Cigarettes)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WNBC)</strong><br>1949-08-23<br>8:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Liggett &amp; Myers</strong><br>Chesterfield Cigarettes</td>\n<td><strong>Dean Martin</strong>, <strong>Jerry Lewis</strong>, <strong>Billie Burke</strong> (Guest)</td>\n<td><strong>Ed Simmons &amp; Norman Lear</strong>, <strong>Dick Stabile Orchestra</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;, &quot;Slapstick&quot;, &quot;Vocal&quot;, &quot;Music&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Dean Martin and Jerry Lewis welcome fluttery stage and screen legend Billie Burke (the Good Witch Glinda in <em>The Wizard of Oz</em>), who becomes hopelessly confounded by Jerry&#39;s high-decibel antics and babysitting schemes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Fitch_1942_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Tuffy Green Fitch</code>, <code>Fitch Bandwagon 1942</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Fitch Bandwagon: Tuffy Green and his Orchestra (Fitch Shampoo)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Red (WEAF)</strong><br>1942-08-23<br>7:30 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>The F.W. Fitch Co.</strong><br>Fitch&#39;s Dandruff Remover Shampoo</td>\n<td><strong>Tuffy Green and his Orchestra</strong>, <strong>Toby Reed</strong> (MC)</td>\n<td><strong>Ward Byron</strong> (Producer), <strong>Toby Reed</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;, &quot;Swing&quot;, &quot;Jazz&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Toby Reed hops aboard the Fitch Bandwagon to showcase popular Midwest dance band leader Tuffy Green and his Orchestra, playing soaring big band swing standards and romantic wartime jump tunes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Doughboys_1946_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Rosika The Rose</code>, <code>Light Crust Flour WBAP</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Rosika, The Rose (The Light Crust Doughboys — WBAP Fort Worth)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>WBAP / TQN</strong><br>1946-08-23<br>12:30 PM CST</td>\n<td><strong>Burrus Mill</strong><br>Light Crust Flour</td>\n<td><strong>The Light Crust Doughboys</strong> (Jim Boyd, Parker Willson)</td>\n<td><strong>Burrus Mill / WBAP</strong>, <strong>Parker Willson</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Country&quot;, &quot;Western Swing&quot;, &quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Folk&quot;, &quot;String Band&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Pioneer Western swing innovators The Light Crust Doughboys deliver toe-tapping fiddle tunes and string band harmonies on WBAP Fort Worth, featuring their spirited version of &#39;Rosika, The Rose&#39; for Light Crust Flour.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Howard_Miller_1955_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Vic Damone 1955</code>, <code>Howard Miller WBBM Chicago</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Howard Miller Show with Guest Vic Damone (CBS Radio — WBBM Chicago)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WBBM)</strong><br>1955-08-23<br>10:45 AM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Wrigley&#39;s Gum</strong><br>Wrigley&#39;s Spearmint Gum</td>\n<td><strong>Howard Miller</strong> (Host / DJ), <strong>Vic Damone</strong> (Guest)</td>\n<td><strong>WBBM Chicago / CBS Radio Presentation</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Interview&quot;, &quot;Pop&quot;, &quot;Easy Listening&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>America&#39;s top disc jockey Howard Miller interviews handsome pop crooner Vic Damone at the WBBM Chicago studios, spinning Vic&#39;s latest chart-topping hits and discussing his upcoming nightclub appearances and Columbia records.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 23</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 23</strong>: 372</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
