    "html": "<h2>August 23 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 46)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 23</strong> across American and British network broadcasting history (1943–1985):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 23 Archival Inventory (Increment 46)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>21st_Precinct_1956_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>[Preempted]</code>, <code>RNC Preemption 1956</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;21st Precinct [Preempted for CBS Republican National Convention Coverage]&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WCBS)</strong><br>1956-08-23<br>8:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>CBS News Special</strong><br>Gavel-to-Gavel Coverage</td>\n<td><strong>Walter Cronkite</strong>, <strong>Edward R. Murrow</strong>, <strong>Everett Sloane</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS News Bureau</strong>, Live from Cow Palace, San Francisco</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Police Procedural&quot;, &quot;Docudrama&quot;, &quot;News&quot;, &quot;Historical&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>The scheduled dramatization of <em>21st Precinct</em> (Captain Frank Kennelly) is preempted by CBS Radio for live wall-to-wall gavel news coverage of the 1956 Republican National Convention from the Cow Palace in San Francisco as President Dwight D. Eisenhower accepts his renomination.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Radio_Active_1982_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Charity Radiothon</code>, <code>Radio Active Radiothon</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Radio Active: The 24-Hour Charity Radiothon (Series 2, Episode 2 — BBC Radio 4)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Radio 4</strong><br>1982-08-23<br>6:30 PM</td>\n<td><strong>Non-commercial</strong><br>BBC Radio Comedy</td>\n<td><strong>Angus Deayton</strong> (Mike Channel), <strong>Geoffrey Perkins</strong>, <strong>Helen Atkinson-Wood</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Angus Deayton &amp; Geoffrey Perkins</strong>, <strong>Jimmy Mulville</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Satire&quot;, &quot;British&quot;, &quot;Parody&quot;, &quot;Sketch&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Local commercial station Radio Active stages an agonizingly inept 24-hour charity radiothon, featuring shameless pledge drives by smooth-talking anchorman Mike Channel, disastrous celebrity phone-ins, and absurd musical appeals.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Radio_Active_1985_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Did You Catch It</code>, <code>Radio Active Series 5 Ep 7</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Radio Active: &#39;Did You Catch It?&#39; Arts Review Parody (Series 5, Episode 7)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Radio 4</strong><br>1985-08-23<br>6:30 PM</td>\n<td><strong>Non-commercial</strong><br>BBC Radio Comedy</td>\n<td><strong>Angus Deayton</strong>, <strong>Geoffrey Perkins</strong>, <strong>Helen Atkinson-Wood</strong>, <strong>Philip Pope</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Angus Deayton &amp; Geoffrey Perkins</strong>, <strong>Jamie Rix</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Satire&quot;, &quot;British&quot;, &quot;Parody&quot;, &quot;Sketch&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>The Radio Active team mercilessly lampoons television arts review programmes and highbrow cultural panels in a pretentious media discussion show titled &#39;Did You Catch It?&#39;, hosted by the pompous Mike Channel.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Counterspy_1943_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Sabotaged Sea Wall</code>, <code>Mail Pouch Counterspy</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Case of the Sabotaged Sea Wall (Mail Pouch Tobacco)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Blue Network (WJZ)</strong><br>1943-08-23<br>9:00 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Mail Pouch Tobacco</strong><br>Mail Pouch &amp; Kentucky Club</td>\n<td><strong>Don MacLaughlin</strong> (David Harding), <strong>Mandel Kramer</strong> (Harry Peters)</td>\n<td><strong>Phillips H. Lord</strong> (Creator), <strong>Bob Shepherd</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Espionage&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;, &quot;Thriller&quot;, &quot;War&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Chief Counterspy David Harding and operative Harry Peters foil an Axis espionage plot to blast an East Coast coastal shipping breakwater and destroy a secret wartime munitions convoy staging area.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Counterspy_1949_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Bouncing Bank Robber</code>, <code>Counterspy Pepsi 1949</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Case of the Bouncing Bank Robber (Starring Don MacLaughlin — Pepsi-Cola)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WNBC)</strong><br>1949-08-23<br>7:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Pepsi-Cola Company</strong><br>Pepsi-Cola (&quot;Twice as much!&quot;)</td>\n<td><strong>Don MacLaughlin</strong> (David Harding), <strong>Mandel Kramer</strong> (Harry Peters)</td>\n<td><strong>Phillips H. Lord</strong> (Producer), <strong>Leonard Bass</strong>, <strong>Don Lowe</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Espionage&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;, &quot;Thriller&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>David Harding and Harry Peters track an athletic criminal syndicate utilizing acrobatic specialized entry techniques and stolen government blueprints to tunnel into high-security federal reserve vault banks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Story_Shop_1947_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Audition</code>, <code>NBC Story Shop Pilot 1947</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The NBC Story Shop: Audition Pilot Special (Morton Wishengrad)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WNBC)</strong><br>1947-08-23<br>1:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>NBC Dramatic Workshop</td>\n<td><strong>Mercedes McCambridge</strong>, <strong>John McIntire</strong>, <strong>Karl Swenson</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Morton Wishengrad</strong> (Writer), <strong>Edward King</strong>, <strong>Ed Herlihy</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;, &quot;Literature&quot;, &quot;Experimental&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>In this audition pilot for a proposed NBC dramatic showcase, an ensemble of radio actors dramatize contemporary American short stories, exploring the moral choices facing post-war veterans returning to civilian small-town life.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 23</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 23</strong>: 276</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
