    "html": "<h2>August 23 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 40)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 23</strong> across American and public broadcasting history (1937–1985):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 23 Archival Inventory (Increment 40)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>ONeill_1937_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>The Straw</code>, <code>Eugene O&#39;Neill Straw</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Eugene O&#39;Neill&#39;s The Straw (Starring Peggy Wood &amp; Frank Lovejoy)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Blue (WJZ)</strong><br>1937-08-23<br>9:30 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>NBC Cultural Drama</td>\n<td><strong>Peggy Wood</strong> (Eileen Carmody), <strong>Frank Lovejoy</strong>, <strong>Joseph Bell</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Eugene O&#39;Neill</strong> (Author), <strong>Lester Vail</strong>, <strong>Frank Black</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;, &quot;Literature&quot;, &quot;Tragedy&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>In Eugene O&#39;Neill&#39;s tragic medical romance <em>The Straw</em>, an impoverished young woman convalescing at a tuberculosis sanatorium falls deeply in love with an ambitious newspaperman whose hope gives her the courage to fight for life.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Line_Up_1951_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Fluffed Fraternal Fidelity</code>, <code>Line Up 1951</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Fluffed Fraternal Fidelity Case (Starring Bill Johnstone &amp; Wally Maher)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WCBS)</strong><br>1951-08-23<br>9:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>CBS Mystery Presentation</td>\n<td><strong>Bill Johnstone</strong> (Lt. Guthrie), <strong>Wally Maher</strong> (Sgt. Grebb)</td>\n<td><strong>Blake Edwards &amp; Morton Fine</strong> (Writers), <strong>Jaime del Valle</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Police Procedural&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Lieutenant Ben Guthrie and Sergeant Matt Grebb of the San Francisco Police Department investigate the execution-style murder of a fraternal lodge treasurer whose safe deposit box was cleaned out during a midnight break-in.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Minds_Eye_1983_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>The Mind&#39;s Eye</code>, <code>NPR Minds Eye 1983</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Mind&#39;s Eye: Soundscapes of Consciousness (NPR Radio Drama)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NPR</strong><br>1983-08-23<br>8:00 PM</td>\n<td><strong>CPB &amp; NEA</strong><br>Public Broadcasting</td>\n<td><strong>Bob Sessions</strong>, <strong>John Baddeley</strong>, <strong>Kerry Shale</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Erik Bauersfeld</strong> (Producer), <strong>Bob Lewis</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Experimental&quot;, &quot;Science Fiction&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>A visionary radio soundscape exploring cognitive perception and human consciousness through experimental binaural audio sound design, internal monologue, and surreal acoustic dreamscapes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Minds_Eye_1985_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Wind In The Willows</code>, <code>Kenneth Grahame Toad</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Kenneth Grahame&#39;s The Wind in the Willows (Bernard Cribbins &amp; Michael Hordern)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NPR</strong><br>1985-08-23<br>8:00 PM</td>\n<td><strong>CPB</strong><br>Public Broadcasting</td>\n<td><strong>Bernard Cribbins</strong> (Toad), <strong>Michael Hordern</strong>, <strong>Ian Carmichael</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Kenneth Grahame</strong> (Author), <strong>John Wells</strong>, <strong>Simon Brett</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Fantasy&quot;, &quot;Children&#39;s&quot;, &quot;Literature&quot;, &quot;British&quot;, &quot;Adventure&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Adapted from Kenneth Grahame&#39;s riverbank classic <em>The Wind in the Willows</em>, Rat, Mole, and Badger attempt to cure the reckless, motorcar-obsessed Mr. Toad before his wild escapades lead to the loss of Toad Hall.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Opry_1958_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Porter Wagoner</code>, <code>Grand Ole Opry 1958</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Grand Ole Opry: Porter Wagoner &amp; The Wagonmasters (Prince Albert Tobacco)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>WSM Nashville</strong><br>1958-08-23<br>8:30 PM CST</td>\n<td><strong>R.J. Reynolds Tobacco</strong><br>Prince Albert Pipe Tobacco</td>\n<td><strong>Porter Wagoner</strong>, <strong>Norma Jean</strong>, <strong>Wagonmasters</strong>, <strong>Minnie Pearl</strong></td>\n<td><strong>WSM Opry</strong>, <strong>Grant Turner</strong> (Announcer), Live Ryman Auditorium</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Country&quot;, &quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Live&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;, &quot;Bluegrass&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>The &quot;Thin Man from West Plains&quot; Porter Wagoner and his Wagonmasters headline the Prince Albert segment live from Nashville&#39;s Ryman Auditorium, performing &#39;A Satisfied Mind&#39; and &#39;Dear Lonesome&#39; alongside country comedy by Cousin Minnie Pearl.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Opry_1969_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Woman Of The World</code>, <code>Loretta Lynn Opry</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Grand Ole Opry: Loretta Lynn Performs &#39;Woman of the World&#39; (Martha White)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>WSM Nashville</strong><br>1969-08-23<br>9:00 PM CDT</td>\n<td><strong>Martha White Mills</strong><br>Martha White Flour &amp; Corn Meal</td>\n<td><strong>Loretta Lynn</strong>, <strong>Ernest Tubb &amp; Texas Troubadours</strong></td>\n<td><strong>WSM Opry</strong>, <strong>Grant Turner</strong> (Announcer), Live Ryman Auditorium</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Country&quot;, &quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Live&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;, &quot;Bluegrass&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Country music superstar Loretta Lynn takes the Ryman Auditorium stage to perform her smash hit single &#39;Woman of the World (Leave My World Alone)&#39; and &#39;Fist City&#39;, accompanied by Texas Troubadour Ernest Tubb for Martha White Flour.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 23</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 23</strong>: 240</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
