    "html": "<h2>August 23 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 25)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 23</strong> across American network and syndication broadcasting history (1937–1951):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 23 Archival Inventory (Increment 25)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Burns_Allen_1937_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Episode 20</code>, <code>Gracies Pet Bird</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Gracie&#39;s Insulting Runaway Parrot (Grape-Nuts Flakes)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Red (WEAF)</strong><br>1937-08-23<br>8:00 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>General Foods Corp.</strong><br>Grape-Nuts &amp; Grape-Nuts Flakes</td>\n<td><strong>George Burns</strong>, <strong>Gracie Allen</strong>, <strong>Tony Martin</strong> (Tenor), <strong>Henry King Orchestra</strong></td>\n<td><strong>George &amp; Willie Burns</strong> (Writers), <strong>Ken Niles</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Sitcom&quot;, &quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Gracie Allen drives George crazy when she adopts a runaway parrot that only recites insults about George&#39;s singing voice, while Tony Martin serenades listeners with romantic hit ballads backed by Henry King&#39;s Orchestra.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>FBI_Peace_War_1945_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Stolen Payroll Heist</code>, <code>Lava Soap FBI 1945</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The War Plant Payroll Robbery (Lava Soap)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WABC)</strong><br>1945-08-23<br>8:30 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Procter &amp; Gamble</strong><br>Lava Heavy Duty Soap</td>\n<td><strong>Martin Blaine</strong> (Agent Sheppard), <strong>Craig McDonnell</strong>, <strong>Ralph Bell</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Frederick L. Collins</strong> (Author), <strong>Max Marcin</strong> (Director)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Police&quot;, &quot;Procedural&quot;, &quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;True Crime&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Federal Bureau of Investigation special agents trace marked bank notes to capture an armed gang of war plant payroll robbers hiding out in an abandoned mountain quarry cabin.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>FBI_Peace_War_1951_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Tip-off Man</code>, <code>Underworld Tip-Off Man</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Underworld Tip-Off Man (Lava Soap &amp; Wildroot Cream-Oil)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WCBS)</strong><br>1951-08-23<br>8:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Lava Soap &amp; Wildroot</strong><br>Lava Soap &amp; Wildroot Cream-Oil</td>\n<td><strong>Martin Blaine</strong> (Agent Sheppard), <strong>Walter Kinsella</strong>, <strong>Mandel Kramer</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Jacques Finke</strong> (Writer), <strong>Len Sterling</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Police&quot;, &quot;Procedural&quot;, &quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;True Crime&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>FBI agents investigate a string of precision jewelry warehouse heists, discovering that a trusted insurance claims adjuster is secretly the underworld &#39;tip-off man&#39; providing safe combinations to safe-cracking crews.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Blue_Beetle_1940_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Dancing Ghost Part 2</code>, <code>Frank Lovejoy Dan Garret</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Dancing Ghost of Rocking Hill (Part 2) — Starring Frank Lovejoy&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Frank Ferrin Syndication</strong><br>1940-08-23<br>(Transcribed)</td>\n<td><strong>Local Commercials</strong><br>Co-op Advertisers</td>\n<td><strong>Frank Lovejoy</strong> (The Blue Beetle), <strong>Joan Alexander</strong>, <strong>Humphrey Davis</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Charles Nicholas</strong> (Creator), <strong>Frank Ferrin</strong> (Producer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Superhero&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;, &quot;Adventure&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>In Part 2 of <em>The Dancing Ghost of Rocking Hill</em>, rookie patrolman Dan Garret downs mysterious Vitamin 2X to transform into the hooded crimefighter The Blue Beetle, investigating eerie glowing apparitions on Rocking Hill to uncover an extortion gang&#39;s counterfeiting laboratory.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Fu_Manchu_1939_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Grebble Smith Prisoners</code>, <code>Si-Fan Captives 63</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Si-Fan Captives in the Thames Dungeon (Episode 63)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Radio Attractions</strong><br>1939-08-23<br>(Transcribed Ep 63)</td>\n<td><strong>Local Commercials</strong><br>Syndicated Advertisers</td>\n<td><strong>Hanley Stafford</strong> (Dr. Fu Manchu), <strong>Gale Gordon</strong>, <strong>Paula Winslowe</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Sax Rohmer</strong> (Author), <strong>Frank Bingman</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Adventure&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Thriller&quot;, &quot;Pulp&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>In Episode 63 of <em>The Shadow of Fu Manchu</em>, Scotland Yard commissioner Sir Denis Nayland Smith and inspector Grebble are captured by the Si-Fan secret society in an underground Thames dungeon, facing Fu Manchu&#39;s deadly venomous spiders while struggling to free themselves from iron shackles.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Ripley_1945_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Believe It or Not 1945</code>, <code>Toni Home Wave Ripley</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Believe It or Not with Robert L. Ripley (Toni Home Wave)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WABC)</strong><br>1945-08-23<br>10:00 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Toni Home Permanent</strong><br>Toni Home Wave Kit</td>\n<td><strong>Robert L. Ripley</strong> (Host), <strong>Linda Keene</strong>, <strong>Ray Bloch and his Orchestra</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Charles Powers</strong> (Director), <strong>Frank Gallop</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Variety&quot;, &quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Docudrama&quot;, &quot;Oddities&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Renowned globetrotter Robert L. Ripley presents incredible <em>Believe It or Not</em> dramatic accounts of human survival and bizarre historical coincidences, interspersed with smooth vocal performances from Linda Keene and orchestra numbers from Ray Bloch.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 23</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 23</strong>: 150</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
