    "html": "<h2>August 23 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 19)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 23</strong> across American and British broadcasting history (1932–1955):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 23 Archival Inventory (Increment 19)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>American_Challenge_1942_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Death of Tad Lincoln</code>, <code>Wishengrad Lincoln Drama</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Death of Tad Lincoln (Morton Wishengrad Dramatization)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Red (WEAF)</strong><br>1942-08-23<br>4:30 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Wartime Morale Drama</td>\n<td><strong>Raymond Edward Johnson</strong> (Lincoln), <strong>Barbara Weeks</strong>, <strong>Tommy Donnelly</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Morton Wishengrad</strong> (Writer), <strong>Homer Fickett</strong> (Director)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Historical&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Biographical&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>A historical dramatization of the White House in the 1860s, depicting President Abraham Lincoln and first lady Mary Todd Lincoln as they endure the devastating fever and untimely death of their beloved young son Tad Lincoln amidst the turmoil of the Civil War.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Gold_Coast_Rhythm_1949_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>George Left Out</code>, <code>AFRS Big George Miller</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;AFRS Gold Coast Rhythm Show: Big George Miller (Louis Jordan &amp; Wynonie Harris)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>AFRS Hollywood</strong><br>1949-08-23<br>(AFRS Disc)</td>\n<td><strong>Non-commercial</strong><br>AFRS Troop Radio</td>\n<td><strong>Big George Miller</strong> (Host / DJ)</td>\n<td>AFRS Hollywood Production</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Jazz&quot;, &quot;Rhythm and Blues&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>AFRS disc jockey Big George Miller spins scorching rhythm and blues sides for overseas servicemen, joking with GI listeners about feeling left out of Hollywood parties while dropping records by Louis Jordan, Wynonie Harris, and Dinah Washington.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Lincoln_Highway_1941_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Guest - Gladys George</code>, <code>Roadside Diner Romance</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Roadside Diner Along U.S. 30 (Starring Gladys George — Shinola)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Red (WEAF)</strong><br>1941-08-23<br>11:00 AM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>Hecker Products</strong><br>Shinola Shoe Polish &amp; 2-in-1 Wax</td>\n<td><strong>Gladys George</strong> (Guest Star), <strong>John McIntire</strong>, <strong>Karl Swenson</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Bate Greer</strong> (Writer), <strong>Theodora Yates</strong> (Director), <strong>Ed Herlihy</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;, &quot;Romance&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Stage and screen star Gladys George portrays a worldly-wise roadside diner owner along U.S. Route 30 who helps a desperate young runaway couple reconcile with their estranged families and start an honest new life.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Adventurers_Club_1947_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Norman Gerlach Story</code>, <code>Congo Safari Elephant</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Belgian Congo Safari Expedition (Norman Gerlach)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mutual (WGN)</strong><br>1947-08-23<br>11:30 AM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Chicago Adventurers Club</td>\n<td><strong>Norman Gerlach</strong> (Himself), <strong>Raymond Edward Johnson</strong>, <strong>Mandel Kramer</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Frank O&#39;Connor</strong> (Writer), <strong>Martin Magner</strong> (Director), <strong>Carl Warren</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Adventure&quot;, &quot;Docudrama&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;, &quot;Travel&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Explorer Norman Gerlach relives his harrowing encounter in the Belgian Congo with the Adventurers Club of Chicago, describing how a rogue rogue elephant herd ambushed his safari expedition deep inside the impenetrable rainforest.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Contraband_1955_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Episode</code>, <code>Cornish Smugglers Cove</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Contraband (Episode 3) — The Cornish Smuggler&#39;s Cove (Berkeley Mather)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BBC Light Programme</strong><br>1955-08-23<br>8:30 PM</td>\n<td><strong>Non-commercial</strong><br>BBC Public Radio</td>\n<td><strong>Patrick Barr</strong> (David Morgan), <strong>Sarah Lawson</strong>, <strong>Geoffrey Keen</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Berkeley Mather</strong> (Writer), <strong>Michael Bakewell</strong> (Producer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Crime&quot;, &quot;Thriller&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;British&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Ex-naval intelligence officer David Morgan goes undercover on the Cornish coast to infiltrate a syndicate smuggling illicit foreign currency and contraband into post-war Britain, navigating treacherous tides and armed smugglers.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Chandu_1932_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Catacombs Alexandria 154</code>, <code>Chandu White King Soap</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Catacombs of Alexandria (Part 154) — White King Soap&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Don Lee / Mutual</strong><br>1932-08-23<br>8:00 PM PST</td>\n<td><strong>Los Angeles Soap Co.</strong><br>White King Granulated Soap</td>\n<td><strong>Gayne Whitman</strong> (Chandu), <strong>Margaret MacDonald</strong>, <strong>Cyril Armbrister</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Harry A. Earnshaw &amp; Raymond Morgan</strong> (Writers), <strong>Howard Culver</strong></td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Fantasy&quot;, &quot;Adventure&quot;, &quot;Occult&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Frank Chandler uses his mystic Eastern powers as Chandu the Magician to penetrate the underground catacombs of Alexandria, battling the illusionary illusions of master villain Roxor to rescue Egyptian Princess Nadji from hypnotic entrapment.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 23</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 23</strong>: 114</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
