    "html": "<h2>August 23 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 08)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, serial multi-part narrative arcs, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 23</strong> across American network and public broadcasting history (1934–2010):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 23 Archival Inventory (Increment 08)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Mercury_1946_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Tell Tale Heart</code>, <code>Welles Im a Fool 1946</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;I&#39;m a Fool (by Sherwood Anderson) &amp; The Tell-Tale Heart (by Edgar Allan Poe)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WCBS)</strong><br>1946-08-23<br>10:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Pabst Brewing Co.</strong><br>Pabst Blue Ribbon (&quot;33 Fine Brews&quot;)</td>\n<td><strong>Orson Welles</strong> (Star / Host), <strong>Keenan Wynn</strong>, <strong>Mercedes McCambridge</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Orson Welles</strong> (Producer &amp; Director), <strong>Bernard Herrmann</strong> (Score)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Horror&quot;, &quot;Literature&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Orson Welles presents a brilliant literary double-bill, first dramatizing Sherwood Anderson&#39;s nostalgic tale of a boastful racetrack swipe in <em>I&#39;m a Fool</em>, followed by Welles&#39;s terrifying, heartbeat-pulsing psychological performance as Edgar Allan Poe&#39;s obsessive murderer in <em>The Tell-Tale Heart</em>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Kraft_1934_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Paul Whiteman Kraft</code>, <code>Miracle Whip 1934</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Paul Whiteman and his Orchestra with Helen Jepson (Kraft Miracle Whip)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Red (WEAF)</strong><br>1934-08-23<br>10:00 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>Kraft-Phenix Cheese</strong><br>Kraft Miracle Whip &amp; Velveeta</td>\n<td><strong>Paul Whiteman</strong> (The King of Jazz), <strong>Helen Jepson</strong> (Soprano), <strong>Jack Fulton</strong></td>\n<td><strong>J. Walter Thompson Agency</strong>, <strong>Ronny Mansfield</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;, &quot;Classical&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>The King of Jazz Paul Whiteman conducts his massive orchestra from New York in symphonic jazz arrangements, featuring operatic soprano Helen Jepson performing &#39;Smoke Gets in Your Eyes&#39; and tenor Jack Fulton for Kraft Miracle Whip.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Kraft_1945_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Waitin For Train</code>, <code>Edward Everett Horton Kraft</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Edward Everett Horton with Guests Johnny Johnston, Kay Armen, and The Charioteers&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WEAF)</strong><br>1945-08-23<br>9:00 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Kraft Foods Company</strong><br>Kraft Miracle Whip &amp; Parkay</td>\n<td><strong>Edward Everett Horton</strong> (Guest Host), <strong>Johnny Johnston</strong>, <strong>Kay Armen</strong>, <strong>Charioteers</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Cal Kuhl</strong> (Producer), <strong>Ken Carpenter</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;, &quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Pop&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Guest host Edward Everett Horton presides over the summer edition of Kraft Music Hall, trading fluttery comedy banter with singing stars Johnny Johnston and Kay Armen on &#39;Waitin&#39; for the Train to Come In&#39; alongside The Charioteers for Kraft Miracle Whip.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Mystery_House_1946_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>The Mummys Curse</code>, <code>Bela Lugosi Mystery House</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Mummy&#39;s Curse (Hosted by Bela Lugosi)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mutual (WOR)</strong><br>1946-08-23<br>9:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Commercial Co-op</td>\n<td><strong>Bela Lugosi</strong> (Host / Mystery Master), <strong>Peter Capell</strong>, <strong>Ann Thomas</strong></td>\n<td><strong>George Lowther</strong> (Writer), <strong>Jock MacGregor</strong> (Director), <strong>Carl Warren</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Horror&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Supernatural&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Horror icon Bela Lugosi introduces a macabre thriller about an archaeologist who transports a sarcophagus from Egypt to a private museum on Long Island, unearthing a series of asphyxiation murders linked to an ancient guardian curse.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Adventure_Parade_1948_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Moby Dick Pt 1of5</code>, <code>Ishmael Signs Pequod</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Moby Dick (Part 1 of 5) — Ishmael and Queequeg Sign Aboard the Pequod&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mutual (WOR)</strong><br>1948-08-23<br>4:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Juvenile Classic Series</td>\n<td><strong>John Griggs</strong> (The Storyteller / Multi-Voice Cast)</td>\n<td><strong>Herman Melville</strong> (Author), <strong>Oliver Barbour</strong> (Adapter &amp; Director)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Adventure&quot;, &quot;Children&quot;, &quot;Literature&quot;, &quot;Maritime&quot;, &quot;Serial&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>In Part 1 of a five-part serialization of Herman Melville&#39;s maritime epic <em>Moby Dick</em>, storyteller John Griggs portrays young Ishmael as he arrives in the whaling port of New Bedford, befriends the tattooed Polynesian harpooner Queequeg, and signs aboard the fateful whaling ship <em>Pequod</em>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Bradbury_Thirteen_2010_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Tomorrows Child</code>, <code>Pyramid Baby Sci-Fi</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Tomorrow&#39;s Child (Adapted from Ray Bradbury)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NPR / BYU Radio</strong><br>2010-08-23<br>(Bradbury Thirteen)</td>\n<td><strong>CPB / BYU</strong><br>Corporation for Public Broadcasting</td>\n<td><strong>Ray Bradbury</strong> (Introduction), <strong>Paul Frees</strong> (Narrator), <strong>Ron Frederickson</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mike McDonough</strong> (Adapter, Producer &amp; Sound Designer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Science Fiction&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;, &quot;Speculative&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Author Ray Bradbury introduces a poignant science-fiction drama about parents whose newborn baby is born into a bizarre fourth dimension due to an experimental hospital birthing machine malfunction, leaving them with the heartbreaking choice of whether to undergo surgery to join their pyramid-shaped child in the other dimension.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 23</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 23</strong>: 48</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
