    "html": "<h2>August 22 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for 6 historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 22</strong> across American network and syndicated broadcasting history (1935–1945):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 22 Exhaustive Archival Inventory</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Charlotte_Greenwood_1944_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Popcorn Cannon</code>, <code>Charlotte Greenwood 1944</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Uncle Joshua&#39;s Exploding Popcorn Cannon&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Blue / ABC (KECA)</strong><br>1944-08-22<br>3:00 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Blue Network Comedy</td>\n<td><strong>Charlotte Greenwood</strong>, <strong>John Brown</strong> (Uncle Joshua), <strong>Harry Bartell</strong> (Mayor), <strong>The King Sisters</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Martin Gosch</strong> (Writer), <strong>Thomas Freebairn-Smith</strong> (Director), <strong>Wendell Niles</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Sitcom&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>High-spirited spinster Charlotte Greenwood attempts to raise funds for the town library by staging a charity bazaar in her barn, only for Uncle Joshua&#39;s homemade popcorn cannon to explode and spray buttered popcorn over the visiting mayor.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>The_Saint_1945_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>G. I. Ghouls</code>, <code>Surplus Penicillin Ring</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;G.I. Ghouls (War Surplus Smugglers)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (KNX)</strong><br>1945-08-22<br>8:00 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Campbell Soup Co.</strong><br>Campbell&#39;s Tomato Soup &amp; V8</td>\n<td><strong>Brian Aherne</strong> (Simon Templar), <strong>Louise Arthur</strong> (Patricia Holm), <strong>Ken Christy</strong> (Inspector Fernack)</td>\n<td><strong>Leslie Charteris</strong> (Creator), <strong>Louis Vittes</strong> (Writer), <strong>Helen Mack</strong> (Director), <strong>Dick Joy</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Simon Templar uncovers a ruthless post-war black market ring hijacking army surplus penicillin and surgical equipment from military warehouses, cornering the gang&#39;s corrupt quartermaster in an abandoned airfield hangar.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>AFRS_Sports_1944_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Joe Hasel Sports Roundup</code>, <code>AFRS Disc 132</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;AFRS Sports Reel: Joe Hasel Military Sports Roundup (Disc 132)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>AFRS Shortwave</strong><br>1944-08-22<br>(Military Broadcast)</td>\n<td><strong>Non-commercial</strong><br>War Dept. Morale Services</td>\n<td><strong>Joe Hasel</strong> (AFRS Sports Correspondent)</td>\n<td><strong>AFRS Los Angeles</strong>, War Department Bureau of Public Relations</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Sports&quot;, &quot;Talk&quot;, &quot;Military&quot;, &quot;Historical&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>AFRS sports commentator Joe Hasel delivers a comprehensive overseas wartime sports broadcast to Allied troops, reviewing the St. Louis Browns&#39; surging American League pennant race and wartime exhibition boxing matches across the Pacific theatre.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Mr_Mrs_North_1945_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Norths Sublet a Murder</code>, <code>Wardrobe Corpse</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Norths Sublet a Murder&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WEAF)</strong><br>1945-08-22<br>8:00 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>The Andrew Jergens Co.</strong><br>Woodbury Facial Soap (&quot;Skin you love to touch&quot;)</td>\n<td><strong>Joseph Curtin</strong> (Jerry North), <strong>Alice Frost</strong> (Pamela North), <strong>Frank Lovejoy</strong> (Lt. Weigand), <strong>Walter Kinsella</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Richard &amp; Frances Lockridge</strong> (Creators), <strong>Hector Chevigny</strong> (Writer), <strong>Ron Rawson</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Detective&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Greenwich Village publisher Jerry North and his delightfully intuitive wife Pam sublet their Manhattan brownstone to an out-of-town art dealer, returning unexpectedly from the country to discover a stabbed stranger concealed inside Pam&#39;s cedar wardrobe.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Words_at_War_1944_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Pastoral Nevil Shute</code>, <code>Words at War 62</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Pastoral (Adapted from Nevil Shute&#39;s Novel)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WEAF)</strong><br>1944-08-22<br>11:30 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Non-commercial</strong><br>Council on Books in Wartime</td>\n<td><strong>Carl Frank</strong> (Narrator), <strong>Karl Swenson</strong> (Flight Lt. Marshall), <strong>Anne Seymour</strong> (Gervase Robertson)</td>\n<td><strong>Nevil Shute</strong> (Author), <strong>Richard McDonagh</strong> (Adapter), <strong>Anton M. Leader</strong> (Director)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;War&quot;, &quot;Romance&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>In an adaptation of Nevil Shute&#39;s wartime novel, a dedicated young RAF Lancaster bomber pilot falls deeply in love with a female WAAF section officer on an English airfield, finding emotional solace while flying perilous nocturnal bombing missions over the Ruhr Valley.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Front_Page_Drama_1935_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Beauty</code>, <code>Vanity Salon Poison</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Beauty (The Vanity Salon Poison Case)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Syndicated</strong><br>1935-08-22<br>(American Weekly)</td>\n<td><strong>Hearst Magazines</strong><br><em>The American Weekly</em> Magazine</td>\n<td><strong>New York Radio Cast</strong></td>\n<td><strong>The American Weekly Radio Staff</strong>, <strong>Roger Bower</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>A dramatic adaptation from the pages of The American Weekly recounting the sensational case of a glamorous Fifth Avenue beauty parlor proprietor targeted with poisoned cosmetic creams by a jealous rival cosmetologist.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 22</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 22</strong>: 116</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
